                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T02:24:34.078116764Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The last five wave-3 gaps in this concern and hist.c, closed. 597 to 602, and the whole suite passes under LC_ALL=C as well as UTF-8, so both charset branches are exercised rather than only the developer's.\n\nEVERY HIST TEST WRITTEN BEFORE THESE RAN AGAINST A BROKEN STASH. hist/test.rs's editor() has never called hist_init, so el_history.buf was empty and sz was 0 — precisely the failed-allocation state ERR-history-11 describes — and nothing noticed. It is measurable: removing the explicit hist_init these tests add gives \"range end index 10 out of range for slice of length 0\". This is the third time the three near-identical editor helpers have disagreed about which inits matter, after search/test.rs needed hist_init for the opposite reason and hid an empty-line bug. A shared, complete test editor is overdue.\n\nFOUR EXPECTATIONS THAT WERE WRONG.\n\nct_decode_argv does not invalidate an old offset by overwriting it. The second call writes only as far as its own content needs, so an offset published by a longer first call still points at untouched, in-bounds, plausible data. Stale-and-plausible is worse than dangling, because nothing faults.\n\nhist_enlargebuf's allocation-failure path IS testable. literal.rs's comments assert OOM paths cannot be reproduced because Rust aborts; that is true of the infallible allocator and false of try_reserve, which returns CapacityOverflow for usize::MAX - 1024. The \"0 with every field unchanged and the old buffer still valid\" guarantee that ch_enlargebufs relies on is directly observable, and now is.\n\nwcwidth in the ASCII charset has no 0 and no 2 — 1 for printable, -1 for everything else. So in the C locale literal_add refuses every literal whose visible character is non-ASCII, dropping the escape and the character together. Both readings are pinned.\n\nERR-history-18's status says the defect is \"still not exercisable ... every reachable call still takes the wide arm\". True across the ABI and false of a unit test, which can set NARROW_HISTORY directly and now does. The register is not edited here.\n\nTWO FUNCTIONS HAVE NO PRODUCTION CALLERS AT ALL. hist_convert (only hist_convert_str is called, from hist_fun) and ct_decode_argv (el_parse deliberately reimplements the loop per-string with ct_decode_string). They compile because of the crate-wide allow(dead_code). Both are rules describing functions the port does not use, which is a question for idiomatization rather than a defect.\n\nA LATENT TRUNCATION IN ElHistoryT. sz duplicates buf.len(), the duplication is load-bearing, and nothing enforces it: hist_enlargebuf tests newsz > sz and then calls buf.resize(newsz, 0), so if sz were ever less than buf.len() the resize truncates the stash and the following fill wipes part of the saved line. Unreachable today — only hist_init, hist_end and hist_enlargebuf write sz and all three keep it equal — but common.rs:122, common.rs:143 and vi.rs:1083 all defensively .min(buf.len()), which is the codebase hedging against an invariant it claims to hold. Dropping sz removes the class.\n\nFOR IDIOMATIZATION. hist_convert's variadic arg is unobservable from Rust: stable Rust can declare `...` but cannot read a VaList, so a test hook can never assert which trailing argument it received and step 2 of the rule is unverifiable. Harmless while every in-tree caller passes NULL. ct_decode_argv cannot be satisfied idiomatically at all — its return is \"one owned Vec whose elements borrow from conv\", which is unrepresentable. literal_get's in-use-prefix guard is not discriminating: an index in l_idx..l_len and one past l_buf.len() both give an empty slice, confirmed by a mutation that was NOT caught, so only the effect is pinned and l_len is pure C bookkeeping. And locale::CHARSET has no test override, so every width-sensitive assertion branches on charset() and pins both readings — a #[cfg(test)] setter would pay for itself across literal, chartype and refresh."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id encoding-idiomatize
                title "Idiomatize the encoding primitives"
                deps (encoding-tests)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:23:01.779087761Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "chartype.rs down 18 production statements, literal.rs up 5, and the 5 are the point.\n\nLITERAL_ADD IS NOW GENUINELY FALLIBLE, and the comment that said it could not be is deleted. It claimed the C's two allocation-failure returns \"cannot be reproduced: Rust's global allocator aborts\". That is false — try_reserve returns CapacityOverflow rather than aborting, which hist_enlargebuf already proved — so reserve_exact became try_reserve_exact and encode_onto gained a try_reserve and a bool. The C restores l_len on realloc failure, so the += 4 now commits after the reservation rather than before, matching it exactly.\n\nA #[cfg(test)] CHARSET OVERRIDE, which pays for itself across four modules. locale::pin_charset is an RAII guard rather than a bare setter, because --test-threads=1 puts every test on one thread and a leaked pin would silently change what later tests read. Verified under one thread in both locales. No production line in locale.rs changed. Every width-sensitive test previously had to branch on charset() and pin both readings, halving what a single run covered.\n\nCT_DECODE_ARGV SHOULD BE RETIRED, and is not deleted. Zero callers and it cannot get one: its C contract is a caller-owned array whose elements borrow from the conversion buffer, and offsets are the nearest safe encoding but an offset is not a pointer, so an ABI shim must rebuild the array anyway — which is what el_parse and el_set's list ops already do, per-string over ct_decode_string. Three C call sites, zero here. Deleting it drops its verify column to unverified, because its /test facet is carried by five tests in chartype.rs and by nothing in driven.rs. So it is a rule re-disposition: retire def, sem and /test together, or re-point them at sem:eln.el-parse-fn, which is where the C's three call sites are actually discharged. Only a doc paragraph was added recording that nothing calls it.\n\nL_LEN EARNS ITS PLACE — keep. It is the only place the C's fixed +4 allocation cadence is visible and sem:literal.literal-add-fn states that cadence, so removing it makes literal_add a bare push with the Vec's geometric growth, which is a behaviour change. sem:literal.literal-clear-fn's early return tests it rather than l_idx, and that asymmetry is the rule's own point. And with try_reserve_exact it is now the REQUESTED capacity while Vec::capacity() is the granted one, so it no longer duplicates anything.\n\nThe finding behind the question is real and is now written in the code: literal_get's idx >= l_idx guard is not discriminating and cannot be — the C's \"uninitialised slot\" and \"past the allocation\" hazards have one answer here — so only the effect is pinned. It stays because it is what keeps l_idx authoritative when a caller drives it out of step with l_buf, which is the only way this port models ERR-terminal-09 at all.\n\nA PRE-EXISTING LC_ALL=C FAILURE, not this node's. history::test::a_saved_history_reloads_into_the_same_entries_in_the_same_order round-trips \"échò\", which cannot survive the ASCII charset. Verified identical at HEAD with the tree stashed. It only appears under --workspace, where feature unification pulls in the bsd path. Belongs to the history concern.\n\nWANTED, BLOCKED ON OTHER CONCERNS. literal_get should take &EditLine — it writes nothing — but the relaxation makes clippy's unnecessary_mut_passed fire in refresh/test.rs. CHTYPE_* should be an enum, not five i32 constants; both ct_visual_width and ct_visual_char carry a dead catch-all arm only because the match is over i32, and refresh.rs matches on them too. ct_encode_char and ct_visual_char should answer Option/Result rather than isize sentinels; callers are in terminal.rs, refresh.rs and keymacro.rs. CtBufferT's csize/wsize are redundant with the Vec lengths and the module doc states the invariant that makes them so, but removing them changes def:chartype.ct-buffer-t and four files. And the two conv_*_resize functions are the same twelve lines twice — the generic shape is obvious, but each wrapper would then be exactly one delegating call over projections of its parameters, which is the trivial-function gate, so it was left rather than gamed."
                    }

