                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:16:20.117007766Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "This node's seventeen tests were written and never claimed. The port gate counts a rule as verified only when a `/test` facet for it appears in a file the target impl's `include` globs match; the tests carried none, so thirteen chared rules and six refresh rules still read as unverified after a commit whose whole purpose was to verify them. Fixed by annotating the existing tests: 402 to 416, no new test code.\n\nThe mechanic, measured rather than assumed, because getting this wrong is silent in both directions. A line of the form `// [spec:libedit:sem:chared.cv-next-word-fn/test]` placed inside a `#[cfg(test)] mod` in a crate source file, directly above the `#[test]` attribute, moves the count by one. The same line without the `/test` suffix moves it by zero. The discriminating experiment was adding exactly one such line above one test and watching 402 become 403.\n\nFourteen of the nineteen came back. The five that did not have no test at all and stay that way rather than being papered over: chared.cv-delfini-fn, chared.c-gets-fn, refresh.re-printstr-fn, refresh.re-putliteral-fn and refresh.re-clear-lines-fn. prompt.prompt-default-fn and prompt.prompt-get-fn are in the same position. Seven rules in this concern remain uncovered and the count now reports that instead of hiding it.\n\nThe lesson generalises to every remaining *-tests node: writing the test is half the job, and the half that does not announce itself when skipped."
                        after_done true
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T02:32:53.245496186Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The last seven gaps in this concern, closed. 602 to 609, green under LC_ALL=C, C.UTF-8 and --test-threads=1, run three times for flake.\n\nNOTHING FAILED AT RUN TIME, which is the weaker result and is worth reading as such — the expectations were traced through both the Rust and the C before being written rather than after. What would have been wrong had any of it been assumed, all now pinned:\n\nterminal_clear_EOL keys off the TERM_CAN_CEOL flag but falls back to spaces when `ce` is absent, so \"CEOL set\" does not imply \"no spaces emitted\". The fallback advances el_cursor.h, which turns the NEXT row's terminal_move_to_char(0) from a no-op into a carriage return. Assuming flag implies capability gives \"\\r\\n\\n\\r\" where the real stream is \"\\r\\n\\n        \\r        \".\n\nre_clear_lines with r_oldcv == 0 is not a no-op on either path: the CEOL loop is an inclusive reverse range so row 0 is still cleared, and the fallback still emits one newline.\n\nre_putliteral's `end` skips a character. literal_add encodes buf[..end] and then buf[end + 1] — buf[end], the closing delimiter, is never encoded. A fixture that puts the delimiter at end + 1 compiles, runs, and interns the wrong bytes silently.\n\ncv_delfini's yank branch leaves the cursor on the anchor in BOTH directions, so a backward yank finishes to the right of the text it copied; only the delete branch has the compensating adjustment. And INSERT alone still deletes, because the delete arm is the else of the YANK test rather than being gated on DELETE.\n\nc_gets writes its cursor blank on every iteration including the terminator one, so the final buffer position holds a space rather than the last character typed.\n\nTHREE SHAPES THAT ARE NOT REACHABLE TODAY AND ARE ONE INVARIANT AWAY FROM BEING BUGS. cv_delfini casts through isize on both the backward yank start and the cursor adjustment; both provably reduce to the pre-motion cursor, but the cast wraps rather than panics if that ever stops holding. re_putliteral indexes el_vdisplay[v][h] directly, so a cursor row past the row count PANICS where the C reads out of bounds — the one place in these three files where a bad coordinate is a panic rather than a defined value, unreachable only because re_nextline clamps. And c_gets clamps the prompt copy to `limit` but sets its cursor unclamped, so a prompt longer than limit leaves the two permanently disagreeing; every store is guarded, so nothing overruns, but backspaces do nothing visible until it falls back under limit.\n\nWHICH INITS EACH HELPER ACTUALLY NEEDED, measured by removing them. chared needs ch_init and read_init: without ch_init, cv_undo and cv_yank clamp to a zero-length buffer, so a delete records an EMPTY KILL BUFFER and every assertion about killed text reads empty; without read_init, el_wpush drops the string and every c_gets test takes the EOF path. It does not need search_init, map_init or hist_init. refresh and prompt need no *_init at all. That is now four helpers with four different answers, which is the argument for one shared editor rather than a fifth.\n\nFOR buffer-idiomatize. CoordT and ElMapCurrent derive nothing — no Debug, no PartialEq — so every position assertion goes through a tuple helper and every mode assertion is assert!(a == b), which prints no diff on failure. Output exists only as bytes on a raw fd with no seam, so terminal, refresh and chared now each carry a near-identical temp-file capture helper; a dyn Write sink on EditLine collapses all three. re_putliteral's (buf, end) pair is the trap above and wants to be (sequence, visible-width). re_printstr is `let _ = (el, str, f);` with zero callers — make it a real tracing hook or delete it and re-disposition the rule. c_gets fuses read, draw and store, so testing the 1008 cap needs 1010 keystrokes pushed through both el_wgetc and re_refresh. cv_delfini's three branches disagree about who moves the cursor and who refreshes, and it returns unit, so nothing in the signature says so. And reaching re_putliteral's allocation-failure arm requires poking l_idx to usize::MAX because LITERAL_INDEX_MAX is private to literal.rs."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id buffer-idiomatize
                title "Idiomatize the line buffer and refresh"
                deps (buffer-tests)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:27:25.234393813Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "+149 net, of which +98 is comment and doc — naming things costs lines. Nothing needed a type another concern owns.\n\nTHE TRAP IS GONE FROM THE SIGNATURE. re_putliteral takes (sequence, visible) rather than (buf, end), so prompt_print passes the slice and the character instead of a slice that had to run two past its meaningful end plus an index into it. The pair literal_add still wants is rebuilt inside re_putliteral, adjacent to the call, with a comment saying the pushed zero occupies the slot `end` names and exists only to satisfy that indexing scheme. Cost is one small Vec per literal per refresh; the C already mallocs per literal in literal_add.\n\nvput is the writing-side twin of the existing cell() reader: one definition of \"write a cell of the virtual image, or nothing if the cursor names a cell that does not exist\", used at all six write sites in re_putc and re_putliteral. It removes the `as usize` on r_cursor — a negative row silently became an enormous index — and the panic where the C read out of bounds.\n\ncv_delfini's signed size and its two wrapping isize casts are gone. The motion is held as the span it names, both ends coming from positions already inside the line, so cursor + size is never formed. The three arms became one expression whose every arm answers the new cursor: a forward delete cannot be the only branch that remembers, and the yank finishing at the anchor — so `yb` ends right of the text it copied — is now a return value rather than an absence. Verified branch for branch against the C, including that c_delbefore's internal clamp can never fire here, which is what made the C's unclamped adjustment correct.\n\nc_gets separated three ways. Keystroke plus c_gets_classify is a pure function of the character and the count, so the 1008 cap is a two-line test rather than 1010 keystrokes through el_wgetc and re_refresh (that test is kept as well). `cp` is deleted — it was always prompt.len() + len, so it is derived at the one place a line index is formed, which is the one place limit is applied, and that closes the clamped-copy-unclamped-cursor trap by leaving no unclamped position to leak. And buf is narrowed once to the largest reachable length, so every store is a direct index and the EL_BUFSIZ - 15 contract is checked once instead of dropped 1009 times; a short slice is now a refused read.\n\nre_clear_lines split into clear_rows_bottom_up and scroll_rows_away over a shared scroll_one_line, so \"clears\" versus \"merely scrolls\" is the function names and ERR-terminal-52 lives in one place.\n\nRE_PRINTSTR IS IMPLEMENTED RATHER THAN STUBBED, and the test changed on purpose. crate::stdio gave el_errfile's descriptor a writer, which was the stub's stated reason. sem:refresh.re-printstr-fn already permits both choices — tracing behind a flag, or omit — so the rule covers the switch unchanged and nothing is ABI-observable, since no call sites are restored: twelve dumps per redraw is a shipped build doing what only a DEBUG_REFRESH one may. The test now verifies the rule's normative byte format instead of the port's choice not to implement it. Deleting the function instead was not available: it would drop the /test facet and take the uncovered count off zero, so that route needs the rule and ERR-terminal-65 edited first.\n\nBLOCKED ON MODULES OUTSIDE THIS CONCERN. literal_add carries the same (buf, end) trap one level down and should take (sequence, visible) and return the pair rather than an out-param — that would also delete the Vec re_putliteral now builds. c_gets's buf should be a fixed-size array rather than a slice, but search.rs passes a subslice. prompt_set and prompt_get's op: i32 should be a side selector, which prompt_set's own doc asks for, but both are called from nshedit-abi. And vput is a check rather than an impossibility; making it unrepresentable means bundling el_vdisplay with r_cursor, and el_vdisplay is a field on EditLine.\n\nLEFT ALONE DELIBERATELY. re_update_line, ~400 lines: twelve i32 offsets into two rows, four phases, and pragmatics whose ordering is load-bearing — fx and sx deliberately not recomputed between the capability blocks, 4c addressing the row in pre-shift coordinates because 4a deferred. It is the function the differential exercises hardest and its rule is step for step; restructuring it is its own wave. re_insert, re_delete and re__strncopy keep their i32 shape because re_update_line passes sizeh as dlen — they bound against the terminal width, not the allocation, and that difference is real. And re_fastputc still writes el_display with `as usize`: the same panic class vput removed, but on the real image from el_cursor, a third cursor-image pairing with its own invariants (ERR-terminal-48), left because extending the guard there without the rule discussing it is the wrong call to make silently."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:27:35.59105328Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        sealed true
    }

