            {
                id input-tests
                title "Write tests for input dispatch and binding from their sem rules; there are none to port"
                deps (input-translate platform-build)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:38:06.53014336Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Every expectation here was read off the Rust and cross-checked against src/read.c and src/parse.c before being written down, so none of them failed on the first run. That is a weaker signal than it sounds — a test that never fails may never have been able to — so two mutations were made and reverted to prove the tests bite: dropping the sibling relink in node__delete fails deleting_one_sibling_leaves_the_rest_of_the_chain, and dropping the restore_handler call in sig_clr fails all three signal tests.\n\nFive values that intuition would have got wrong, recorded because they are what the tests are for.\n\nmap_get_editor on a freshly initialised editor reports vi, not emacs. sem:map.map-get-editor-fn says a fresh EditLine has type == 0 == MAP_EMACS, which is true of the calloc, but map_init ends in map_init_vi because el.h defines VIDEFAULT. The rule describes the struct, not the constructor.\n\n\\U+00041x consumes nine characters, not eight: the fifth hex digit is consumed inside the loop and ERR-input-37's tail still eats one more. \\18 consumes two, not four, because the --p walks back onto the last digit actually taken. M- at end of string is two literal characters rather than an error — the in[2] != '\\0' test fails and it falls through to the verbatim arm twice. \\400 is -1 rather than a truncation.\n\nread_pop removes the front. The C confirms it: el_free(ma->macro[0]) then a shift down. That is what makes the queue FIFO despite the `level` vocabulary, and it is the point of ERR-input-42.\n\nTHE SIGNAL TESTS AND WHY THEY CANNOT KILL THE RUNNER. Dispositions are process-wide and cargo test runs on threads of one process, so the three hold a static Mutex, stepping over poison so a panic in one does not mask another's assertion. Every one uses SIGWINCH, whose default action is ignore, so a test that leaves a disposition unrestored or reaches sig_handler's re-raise cannot take the process down. The untabled-signal case (ERR-terminal-12) uses signal 0, which is not a signal at all and cannot be delivered; any live signal outside the seven would mean betting on a survivable default action on every target.\n\nONE PLACE THE PORT'S FIX IS NOT A SUPERSET OF THE C. sig_clr calls plat::clear_signal_slot() unconditionally, so with two armed EditLines the first to clear unpublishes the second's registration. sem:sig.sig-set-fn already makes serialising the caller's job, exactly as the C does, and this is ERR-terminal-55's territory — but the C never clears `sel` at all, so the port gives it one more way to bite. Documented rather than filed: it is a consequence of having a slot to clear."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:38:12.812999138Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id input-idiomatize
                title "Idiomatize input dispatch and binding"
                deps (input-tests)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:03:45.048656922Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Two production functions added across five files (map.rs help_name, keymacro.rs kprint_leaf); read.rs lost 32 lines. All 149 annotations still attached, no rule bumped.\n\nTHE EXTRACTION THAT MATTERS IS kprint_leaf. node_lookup step 3c and node_enum step 4 held six verbatim identical lines — and among them TWO INDEPENDENT COPIES OF THE ERR-input-05 BOUNDS CHECK, which is the only thing standing between either caller and an out-of-bounds write: ct_visual_char guarantees cnt + used <= KEY_BUFSIZ, while node_enum reserves six wchar_t where a non-BMP non-printable takes eight. One copy now. Two copies of a safety check is one copy waiting to be edited alone.\n\nread.rs's private errno table is gone into crate::errno, which already held four of the nine. A second table of Linux errno numbers in a crate that has a module named for exactly that was the sort of thing that stays wrong quietly.\n\nXK_CMD/XK_STR/XK_NOD were spelled in read.rs and map.rs as well as keymacro.rs, which owns keymacro.h. Both now import. TERMINAL.RS STILL HAS A THIRD COPY at lines 99-102 — not this concern's, and worth folding when something next touches it.\n\nmap_init_meta's two counter-as-sentinel loops became position(): the C expresses \"not found\" as the loop counter reaching 0400, which is Option exactly. The new form is also total where the old one indexed, so an empty key table after map_end answers None instead of panicking — unreachable today because the callers guard on len() == N_KEYS.\n\nTHE VI-INSERT DEFAULT IS NOW LEGIBLE IN FOUR PLACES, because a reader guesses wrong from the struct: type's zero is MAP_EMACS and current's first variant is Key, so the shipped state after map_init looks like emacs and is vi insert. Said on the struct, on both fields, in map_init's doc, and at the one step that decides it. The test now asserts the state directly rather than only through map_get_editor — key['i'] == ED_INSERT against alt['i'] == VI_INSERT is the crisp tell.\n\nTHE sig_clr FIX NEEDS A FUNCTION IN nshedit-plat AND CANNOT BE DONE HERE. sig_clr calls clear_signal_slot() unconditionally, so with two armed EditLines the first to clear unpublishes the second's registration. nshedit-plat::signal exposes only set and clear; PENDING_SLOT is a private AtomicPtr with no reader, so sig.rs cannot ask whether the registration is still its own. The fix is one function there — clear_signal_slot_if(slot) doing a compare_exchange against null, with sig_clr passing &raw const sig.sig_no — which makes editor A's clear a no-op while B holds the slot. Sound in every order: if B ended first the slot is null and the CAS no-ops; if A's box is dropped the slot never pointed at it. It removes the port-specific extra bite and does NOT close ERR-terminal-55's arming race, which sem:sig.sig-set-fn already assigns to the caller exactly as the C does.\n\nSEVEN COPIES OF upto_nul, AND THE RIGHT HOME ALREADY EXISTS. keymacro.rs has two, map.rs two, parse.rs one, plus search.rs, terminal.rs and tty.rs. chartype.rs:95 already has the generic `fn upto_nul<T: Copy + Default + PartialEq>` — private. Making it pub(crate) deletes all seven. Same shape as the fifteen output sinks and the eight CtBufferT literals.\n\nparse__escape and parse__string were left as index cursors deliberately, and this is the interesting negative result. The CONSUMED COUNT is half their contract — \\U+00041x consuming nine, \\18 consuming two — and every idiomatic rewrite tried (char iterator, Peekable, per-form helpers) either hides that count or splits ERR-input-37's over-consumption away from the escape that earns it. All four pinned edges re-verified rather than assumed.\n\ndriven.rs is stale and was already stale before this wave: it embeds file:line for every rule the conformance drivers cover, coverage.sh --check is in neither run.sh nor cargo test --workspace nor nplan check, and four concerns are shifting lines concurrently. Regenerate once, after all four land."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:03:58.507609972Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        sealed true
