            {
                id history-tests
                title "Write tests for history and search from their sem rules; there are none to port"
                deps (history-translate history-regex conformance-differential)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T01:41:27.326760985Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Thirty-eight tests, ten rules. The two left uncovered — hist.hist-enlargebuf-fn and hist.hist-convert-fn — are in hist.rs, which history-listing-escape held at the time.\n\nFIVE EXPECTATIONS THAT WERE WRONG, which is what these were for.\n\nc_setpat at column zero does not always give the empty pattern. In vi command mode EL_CURSOR adds one unconditionally, so cursor 0 on a non-empty line yields a one-character pattern; only emacs can produce the empty one.\n\n`f` then `t` does not demonstrate ERR-modes-64. After `f` the cursor is ON the target, so the never-re-find-the-character-under-the-cursor skip fires and `t` advances. The defect needs `t` followed by `t` or `;`, where the cursor sits one before the target and the skip never fires.\n\nhist/test.rs's editor() is not sufficient for search. It omits hist_init, and ce_inc_search's abort path calls hist_get with eventno == 0, which restores the line from el_history.buf — with sz == 0 that silently EMPTIES the line rather than restoring it, so the rollback test read as \"the search deleted the user's text\". It also omits map_init/map_init_emacs, without which every keystroke dispatches as command 0 and the pattern never grows.\n\ncv_search's reuse path (ERR-modes-36) is observably wrong rather than cosmetic: reusing a stored `abc` yields `.*ab.*`, which matches a history entry `abx` that the correct `.*abc.*` would not.\n\nWHAT FOUGHT THE TESTS STRUCTURALLY, which is the input to history-idiomatize and is more valuable than the count.\n\nhistory_def_delete and history_def_insert are DEAD in production. Every real caller goes through the _raw forms; the two safe wrappers exist only so the rules have a Rust function of that name. Testing the named function tests argument forwarding plus a body nothing calls. The raw/safe split bought nothing and cost the rule its meaning.\n\nThe builtin store cannot be constructed or released idiomatically: history_def_init writes through a &mut *mut c_void out-parameter and HistoryTGen has no Drop, so teardown is history_def_clear plus free_alloc and every test writes its own RAII wrapper. Results are raw *const C that expire on the next call, so every test needs a walker and must snapshot eagerly. is_def_next makes \"is this the builtin?\" a function-pointer identity comparison, so testing a custom function set means writing ten extern \"C\" functions.\n\nhistory_load opens the file itself while history_save_out takes &mut dyn Write. The save half round-trips in memory; the load half needs a real temp file. Splitting load the same way would put the whole round trip in memory.\n\nsearch.rs: PCHAR is a thread-local, so state leaks between calls within a test and isolation depends on #[test] running per-thread, with nothing in the signature saying so — it belongs on ElSearchT. ce_inc_search implements backspace by RECURSION, so no intermediate level is observable and every claim about a middle state is inferred. It draws its prompt into el_line.buffer and unwinds it with a newline-seeking strip_prompt, so display and data are the same array. c_hmatch takes *const u32, which forces common.rs to build a NUL-terminated copy of every candidate on every comparison. ce_search_line takes its pattern implicitly from patbuf[LEN..], an unstated precondition a test must construct by hand. cv_search can only get input through c_gets, which also repaints and destroys the line, so \"did the search work\" and \"was the line destroyed\" cannot be separated. cv_csearch has five positional parameters with two in-band sentinels.\n\nTHERE IS NO SHARED TEST EDITOR and there should be. blank_editline hands back an object no subsystem has touched, so each module rebuilds map_init + map_init_emacs + ch_init + search_init + hist_init + read_init + a hand-sized display. Three near-identical copies now exist in hist/test.rs, vi/test.rs and search/test.rs, and they already disagree about which inits matter — which is exactly how the empty-line bug above hid."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T01:41:36.218868298Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
