            {
                id history-idiomatize
                title "Idiomatize history and search"
                deps (history-tests)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-07T00:46:16.823287463Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "SCOPE, decided before any code was written, because this node carries only a title and 4,688 lines admit several readings.\n\nTHE SEAM IS NARROW AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE REST FREE. nshedit-abi touches history.rs through exactly six names: history_gen, history_init, history_end, history_winit, history_wend, history_save_fd, plus the HistoryGen type. Everything behind those is invisible to a C caller, and dec:libedit:idiomatic-core binds conformance at the ABI boundary rather than inside the core, so the core's representation is not something a defect can be \"reproduced\" in.\n\nWHAT IS BEING DONE. Both halves, not just the internals.\n\n1. The builtin store becomes idiomatic. HentryGen's intrusive circular list — raw next/prev pointers into a fake sentinel header, a cursor that is a pointer compared against &list for \"no current event\" — becomes owned entries in a VecDeque with an Option<usize> cursor. HistoryTGen's max/cur/eventid/flags become typed fields. That removes very nearly all the unsafe in the file.\n\n2. The opcode dispatch and the vtable MOVE TO nshedit-abi. history_gen's H_* match, HistoryGen's ten Option<fn> slots plus its *mut c_void h_ref, and HistEventPrivateGen — the layout-compatible twin that exists only so history_def_add can get a mutable handle by casting — are compatibility artifacts in the C itself. The decision's own words: everything the C exposes that Rust would never choose lives in the ABI crate and is frozen there. The core is then left with History<C> and methods.\n\nH_FUNC IS THE REASON THE VTABLE CANNOT SIMPLY BE DELETED. A C caller can install eleven function pointers and replace the store wholesale, and that is a real exported feature, not decoration. It survives — in nshedit-abi, where the pointers belong, with the builtin store adapted to it by extern \"C\" shims rather than being built out of it.\n\nWHAT IS NOT IN SCOPE. search.rs. It is a separate concern with its own C shape, and the interactive paths it owns are covered only by the pty differential, which localises a failure poorly. It gets its own pass.\n\nORDER, and why it is not negotiable. history-tests lands first. This node's dep on it is real: the conformance differential proves the ABI boundary and says nothing about the core's internals, which is exactly what is being replaced here. Twelve rules had no test at all and the store's eviction order, event-id allocation, H_APPEND anchoring and H_UNIQUE handling are precisely the behaviours a VecDeque rewrite would get subtly wrong in a way no driver would notice."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T08:36:04.248499838Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "THE PHASE 3 FORK IS RESOLVED, and the seam is built ahead of the refactor so that phase is a pure move rather than a move plus a redesign.\n\nTHE FORK. Moving history_gen and the H_FUNC vtable into nshedit-abi is what the chosen scope asks for, and hist_command stood in the way: for `history size` and `history unique` in an .editrc, the C calls history_w(el->el_history.ref, &ev, op, num) DIRECTLY, bypassing the installed dispatcher and reinterpreting the opaque cookie as libedit's own wide store (ERR-history-05). Naming that store means naming history_gen, and nshedit cannot depend on nshedit-abi. So the core had a call it could not keep and could not move.\n\nTHE RESOLUTION. HistSource::CAbi gains a third field, `settings: Option<HistSettingsT>`, where HistSettingsT is `unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void, c_int, c_int) -> c_int`. Non-variadic deliberately: the C's version carries one int and nothing else, so there is no reason to inherit a `...` stable Rust cannot define. nshedit-abi installs it beside the dispatcher at both entry points — el_wset's EL_HIST arm and eln's narrow one — and the pun happens in the ABI's hist_settings shim, where HistoryW can be named.\n\nhist.rs no longer imports HistoryArg, HistoryW or history_w at all. That unused-import warning was the check: the core is now free of the wide store, so Phase 3 moves history_gen with nothing left pointing back.\n\nWHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT FIX. The recorded hole stays open and the comment says so at both ends. For a CUSTOM store installed through the wide entry point the C is type confused, and it still is here — the shim does the same cast. Closing that needs the store to publish a way to recognise its own handle, which is a different question from where the call lives. Checking is_builtin inside the shim would not do it either: reading h_next off a foreign object to decide whether it is one of ours commits the same undefined read the confusion consists of. Moving the pun must not appear to close a hole it does not close.\n\nBehaviour is unchanged in every reachable case, and all eight conformance stages pass, which is what says so: builtin wide store works, narrow store gets -1 from the NARROW_HISTORY check ahead of the hook, no history gets -1 from the is_attached guard, Rust history answers for itself.\n\nA TRAP FOR THE NEXT EDIT OF crates/nshedit-abi/src/histedit.rs. The shim first landed between el_wset's `/// C:` doc line and its three `// [spec:...]` annotations, which orphaned that doc onto the shim. Nothing failed to compile and no test failed — the only thing that noticed was header-diff.sh, because cbindgen renders that doc comment into the shipped histedit.h and the checked-in copy stopped matching the generator. Its report said \"0 check(s) failed\" while exiting 1, because the drift check sets status early and prints its own message far above the verdict. Read the whole report, not the last line."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:31:10.457192744Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "PHASE ONE ONLY. The store's representation, inside the core. Phase two — moving history_gen and the H_FUNC vtable to nshedit-abi — is untouched and specifically unbroken: HistoryGen's field list and layout are unchanged, so the ABI's stack-local construction still compiles, and replacing is_def_next's function-pointer identity test with a bool field on HistoryGen was rejected for exactly that reason. This node stays open.\n\nTHE NUMBERS. history.rs: 64 unsafe blocks to 39, 18 unsafe fn to 12, 2559 lines to 2399, function count unchanged. hist.rs 15 to 8 and 3 to 1. history/test.rs 10 to 2. Across five files, 689 insertions against 871 deletions.\n\nMore important than the count: every remaining unsafe block in history.rs is a one-line conversion at the C-ABI edge. The six-to-twenty-line raw-pointer bodies — `unsafe { let list = &raw mut (*h).list; ... }` — are all gone.\n\nWHAT THE STORE IS NOW. HistoryTGen is a VecDeque newest-first plus an Option<usize> cursor. The embedded sentinel, the next/prev links on every entry, and `cur` are gone; cur() derives from len(). An entry is { num, cstr: Vec<C>, data } — the text owned and NUL-terminated, so ev->str is cstr.as_ptr(), and a Vec's buffer does not move when the deque reshuffles, which is what keeps the ABI's borrow exactly as long-lived as the C's. Eleven now-pointless string helpers deleted (s_alloc, s_dup, s_cmp_eq, s_at and the rest), and the deliberate leaks — ERR-history-01, -13, -15 — are safe Vec::leak now.\n\nTHE RAW/SAFE SPLIT DISSOLVED RATHER THAN BEING DELETED, which is the better answer to the question the test pass raised. history_def_delete and history_def_insert were dead because an intrusive list cannot promise a unique reference around its own nodes; with indices there is no aliasing problem, so both take &mut HistoryTGen and ARE the real bodies, called from history_def_enter, history_def_del and history_deldata_nth. No rule-bearing function was deleted, none is dead, and the rules mean what they say again. HistoryGen::builtin() pairs is_def_next with the h_ref cast, collapsing eight raw dereferences into one.\n\nhistory_def_init returns the cookie instead of writing through a void**, so a test writes HistoryTGen::new(max) and nothing else — no wrapper, no Drop, no clear-then-free teardown. That deleted a 50-line RAII harness from the tests. history_load split into a thin File::open plus history_load_in over a BufRead, symmetric with history_save_out, so the round trip is entirely in memory.\n\nTHE C-LOCALE FAILURE IS FIXED. The round-trip test pins the charset with the pin_charset guard that landed in the encoding node, so LC_ALL=C now passes 373.\n\nRECOMMENDATION ON hist_convert: RE-ANNOTATE, DO NOT RETIRE. It has no production caller and cannot get one — it exists to hand ct_decode_string's borrow back as a raw pointer, and every in-tree caller wants the slice, which is hist_convert_str. Same shape as ct_decode_argv. Two options: retire def, sem and /test together and re-point the narrow-path behaviour onto hist_fun's NARROW_HISTORY arm; or re-annotate both rules onto hist_convert_str and drop the raw wrapper. The second is preferred and is a one-line change that keeps the verify column, and the C's HIST_FUN macro is the real named thing. Nothing changed.\n\nDROP ElHistoryT::sz, BUT NOT FROM HERE. The invariant sz == buf.len() is real and unenforced, and the bug class is as described. Dropping the field removes it and deletes two defensive .min(buf.len()) hedges. Blocked: sz is read by common.rs and set by fixtures in common/test.rs, vi/test.rs and chared/test.rs — four files across three other concerns. Mechanical, and should be one commit after all concerns are quiet.\n\nA RULE THAT IS RIGHT ABOUT THE C AND MISLEADING FOR A PORT. sem:history.history-def-delete-fn step 3 says deleting any node the cursor is not on leaves the cursor alone. True of the C, which names nodes by address. In an index representation it is half true: removing an entry NEWER than the cursor must slide the cursor down or it silently names a different event. The rule was not touched; a test now pins the direction the rule cannot see, and it is the only path in the new remove() no production caller reaches.\n\nFIVE THINGS BLOCKED ON OTHER MODULES. hist_command should take &[&[u32]] — lib.rs already documents that as the crate convention and NAMES hist_command among the seven that use it, but it is the one that does not, so parse.rs carries a 20-line adapter that builds a pointer array purely to feed it and this change immediately unpacks it again. c_hmatch's *const u32 forces common.rs to allocate a NUL-terminated copy of every candidate on every comparison, inside a history walk. cv_csearch's five positional parameters with two in-band sentinels; el_match's two raw pointers; and PCHAR onto ElSearchT, which is blocked only because the one place an ElSearchT is built is el.rs's struct literal.\n\nSEARCH.RS IS BARELY TOUCHED — eight lines, all doc — and that was the right call under the budget. Two findings there were deliberately declined rather than deferred: ce_inc_search's recursion is where \"no intermediate state is observable\" is LOAD-BEARING, because step 8d reads the outer level's oldpchar to decide whether ^G un-fails or aborts and step 9 skips its restore on CC_REFRESH, so state leaks outward across levels by design — an explicit stack makes all of that observable and is easy to get subtly wrong, and it wants its own node. And the prompt drawn into el_line.buffer is the C's design rather than a translation artifact; separating display from data changes what re_refresh sees."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T12:18:54.652752106Z
