                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T12:25:42.526299476Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "PHASE TWO IS NOT THE RIGHT WORK. The scope decision at the top of this node was written on 2026-08-07, before history-rust-seam and the workspace manifest split changed what it was reasoning about. Recorded rather than executed, with the checks that settled it.\n\nWHAT IT ASKED FOR. Move history_gen's H_* match, HistoryGen's ten Option<fn> slots plus its h_ref, and HistEventPrivateGen into nshedit-abi, leaving the core \"History<C> and methods\".\n\nFIVE FACTS, each checked against the tree rather than argued from the shape.\n\n1. THE DECISION IT CITES NAMES HISTORY AS CORE. plan/decisions/idiomatic-core.md, final paragraph: \"Everything genuinely internal — the line buffer, refresh, terminal, history, the command sets — belongs to the core.\" The clause that sends code the other way is about readline.c, eln.c's narrow wrappers and the varargs dispatch, which are compatibility layers in the C itself. history.c is not one of those.\n\n2. THE COMPATIBILITY FACE IS ALREADY MOVED. history-rust-seam put the va_list reader in crates/nshedit-abi/src/histedit.rs:1671-1830 — it collects the varargs, assembles the stack-local TYPE(History) hf for H_FUNC, and hands the core a HistoryArg. What phase two would move is what is left after that: fn(handle, &mut ev, opcode, HistoryArg) -> i32, with no varargs in it.\n\n3. THE VTABLE CROSSES NO ABI. crates/nshedit-abi/include/histedit.h:208 is `typedef struct historyW HistoryW;` — opaque, exactly as upstream's header has it. No C caller can name a field of HistoryGen, so its layout is frozen by nothing and it is an implementation choice, which fact 1 assigns to the core.\n\n4. HistEventPrivateGen's STATED REASON WAS FALSE. Its doc claimed the type stays \"because a caller reading the C's header will look for it\". The C declares it at src/history.c:138 as an untagged `typedef struct { ... } HistEventPrivate;` at file scope: in no header, and with no struct tag a caller could name even if it were. Corrected in this commit to say what is true, including that #[repr(C)] there is only the structural half of the twin claim — HistEventGen is #[repr(C)] too.\n\n5. MOVING IT WOULD BREAK THE CONSUMER THE CRATE SPLIT EXISTS FOR. nshedit-abi declares rust-version 1.99 for c_variadic and rust-toolchain.toml pins a nightly. The root manifest states the reason the floor is not carried up: \"Carrying its requirement up here made the whole workspace unbuildable on stable, which locked out consumers of the *library* -- the Rust port of dash uses nshedit directly and needs none of the C ABI or its varargs.\" OwnedHistoryW is built directly on HistoryGen and is documented as what makes EditLine::set_history usable with no adapter; the core's histedit.rs re-exports HistoryW and History under def:histedit.history-w and def:histedit.history. All of it would go behind nightly, and with it the only reader of the _HiStOrY_V2_ format — which is what the pending format sniffer needs to keep in the core.\n\nWHERE THE CUT WOULD HAVE LANDED, and why it is the wrong seam. history_load, history_save, the four size/unique accessors and the five prev/next opcodes all take &mut HistoryGen<C> because they dispatch THROUGH the vtable — that is what makes H_FUNC's replaceable store work at all. Cutting between store and vtable would make roughly fifteen private core helpers pub (try_alloc, s_in, s_own, he_seterrev, the fifteen _HE_* codes, HIST_COOKIE) and would separate every sem:history.history-def-*-fn rule's logic from the function it is named for. The store and its vtable are one unit.\n\nThe idiomatic surface phase two was reaching for already exists and is in the core: OwnedHistoryW gives a Rust caller enter, set_size, set_unique, the four walks and exec as the escape hatch, and implements EditorHistory. Nothing about the C dispatcher underneath it is visible to that caller.\n\nWHAT WAS DONE INSTEAD. This node's own deferred item, which phase one blocked on every concern being quiet. They are: the other eight wave-4 nodes are done.\n\nElHistoryT::sz IS GONE. It was exactly buf.len() in every reachable state — hist_init resizes and sets both, its failure path leaves both at zero, hist_end clears both, hist_enlargebuf resizes and sets both — so sz == buf.len() was an invariant with no enforcement and no writer that could break it. Two defensive .min(buf.len()) hedges went with it, in hist_get and hist_save_line, along with a third clamp inside hist_get that was then reading n.min(stash.len()) where n was already bounded by it.\n\nA DEAD BRANCH FELL OUT, which is the argument for the change rather than a bonus. hist_enlargebuf ended with `buf[oldsz..newsz].fill(0)` under a comment saying it was a no-op \"unless hist_init's allocation failed and left a stash shorter than sz claims\". That state is unreachable — hist_init returns before assigning sz — so the fill was zeroing what resize had already zeroed, guarded by a comment describing a case that cannot occur. Both are gone; resize's own fill value is the memset the C does.\n\ndef:hist.el-history-t is unaffected. Its body is `typedef struct el_history_t` and names no field, so there is no divergence and no version bump.\n\nTHE CRATE CONVENTION WAS AMENDED RATHER THAN QUIETLY BROKEN. lib.rs said the C's separate size/capacity fields \"are kept anyway, because the translations refer to them by name\". That reason expired with wave 2. It now says a field that is exactly the container's own length goes, and names the two that stay and why they are not derivable: el_line.limit is buffer.len() - EL_LEAVE, and c_redo.lim deliberately lags a reallocation (ERR-buffer-20). sz was the only field in the first class.\n\n373 tests in both locales, 8/8 conformance stages, header drift empty, 609/614 unchanged."
                    }

