                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T02:14:25.359608623Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "THE UNFINISHED HALF OF THIS NODE, closed now rather than left to be rediscovered. Deleting the vis and unvis bodies moved seventy def and sem annotations onto the `pub use bsd::vis` re-export, and the reasoning written at the time was sound for the two facets a re-export can honestly satisfy. It said nothing about the third, and the third is where it broke.\n\nA /test facet on that re-export would be this tree claiming it verifies an implementation living in another repository — one with its own [spec:libbsd:...] corpus and its own tests. The only ways to reach 650 were to write tests that only run under an optional feature, which reports \"tested\" about code the default build does not compile, or to leave thirty-four rules permanently unverified so the port gate could never go green. Both make the number mean less than it appears to.\n\nSo the rules are retired. docs/spec/port/src/vis.md and unvis.md are deleted, src/vis.c, src/vis.h and src/unvis.c leave the source-impl scan the same way strlcpy.c and the other libc gap-fillers already had, and `nplan port extract` rewrote the manifest from the narrowed globs. 650 symbols becomes 614, and waves 1 and 2 went from 650/650 to 614/614 — still complete, now over a denominator that counts only what this tree implements. Wave 3 went 599/650 to 597/614: the two that disappeared were vis and unvis rules that already had a /test facet, so nothing verified was lost.\n\nNOTHING ABOUT WHAT WE SHIP CHANGES, and that was checked rather than assumed. `pub use bsd::vis` stays — nshedit-abi's readline layer reaches Encoder through it at two sites. And the C ABI never exported these symbols in the first place: nm -D on the built cdylib finds zero of them, which matches Debian's own libedit.so.2 IMPORTING strvis@LIBBSD_0.0 rather than providing it. conformance-abi-shape sees no difference either way.\n\n`nplan spec validate` reports no orphan refs, so no annotation anywhere still points at a rule that stopped existing.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE. The bsd crate's vis is now an untested dependency from this tree's point of view, and that is the correct place for the gap to sit rather than a reason to be comfortable. conformance/vis-cross.sh still cross-checks the in-tree C against libbsd.so, which is evidence about the C and about libbsd but not about the Rust crate we actually link. If that ever matters, the check belongs in the bsd repo where the implementation and its rules live."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id history-listing-escape
                title "Escape the `history` listing for display without needing the whole vis engine"
                priority 1
                kind @feat
                deps (history-record-blob)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T01:37:59.921013837Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "THE BUILTIN WAS DEAD, not merely unescaped. Measured before writing anything: on a default build, hist_command with two entries attached returns -1 and writes zero bytes; with --features bsd the same probe returns 0 and prints both lines. vis_encode is None without the feature and the listing turns that into the C's -1, so `history` and `history list` did nothing at all on the build that ships.\n\nFIVE THINGS A FROM-SCRATCH PORT OF strvis(..., VIS_NL) GETS WRONG, all now pinned.\n\nA newline is \\012, not \\n. makeextralist puts '\\n' in the EXTRA list rather than changing the pass-through test, do_svis therefore refuses to pass it, and do_mbyte's iswextra branch is the octal one. \\n would need VIS_CSTYLE, which this call site does not pass. A backslash is \\134 for the same reason.\n\nA NUL is ALWAYS extra, whatever the flags, because wcschr(extra, L'\\0') finds the list's own terminator. So NUL is \\000 and not the \\^@ its control shape suggests. The C makes this rule entirely by accident and it is the single most likely bug in a reimplementation.\n\nThe test is (c & 0177) == ' ', not c == ' '. Byte 0xA0 shares its low seven bits with a space, so in the C locale it comes out \\240 — one octal escape in the middle of a run of \\M- escapes.\n\nis_extra is per CHARACTER, not per byte: every byte of a multi-byte extra character is octal-escaped.\n\nThe mbrtowc failure latch is global to the call and shared by both loops. `caf\\xff\\xc3\\xa9` in a UTF-8 locale stays `caf\\xff\\xc3\\xa9` — the well-formed é after the bad byte is never offered to the converter again and the whole output goes byte-at-a-time.\n\nVIS_NL OUTPUT IS NOT PRINTABLE ASCII. In UTF-8 every byte 0xA0-0xFF passes through raw. Anything downstream that assumes the listing is ASCII is wrong.\n\nWHY THE CHARSET IS A PARAMETER rather than read from the process. bsd::vis answers to libc's LC_CTYPE, which is C in a Rust test binary because nothing calls setlocale; nshedit::locale answers to the environment, which is UTF-8 here. Without an explicit charset the differential would have compared two different locales and agreed for the wrong reason. Calling setlocale was not available: it is a process-wide side effect on parallel tests and no-c-ffi.md bars the core from naming the symbol.\n\nTHE DIFFERENTIAL IS WEAKER EVIDENCE THAN 793/793 SUGGESTS, and the agent said so rather than letting it stand: bsd/src/vis/encode.rs was read alongside src/vis.c before a line was written, so it partly confirms a transcription rather than catching an independent guess. Three mutations were planted to test the harness instead — dropping the NUL-is-extra rule (caught by 9 corpus inputs), narrowing (c & 0177) == ' ' to c == ' ' (6), and removing the widening latch (1) — each caught. A 275-row expectation table generated from the in-tree src/vis.c in both locales also passed, and was deleted rather than committed.\n\nlibbsd is a sound stand-in for src/vis.c here, checked rather than assumed: the in-tree C compiled standalone and diffed against libbsd over the whole vis corpus plus all 256 single bytes, in both C and C.UTF-8, is byte-identical 314 lines each way.\n\nNOT DONE, and it is the reason nobody noticed. differential.sh never drives the listing: el_api.c parses a `history` line but with no history attached, so the loop body never runs. Wiring that up would have caught a dead builtin years earlier than a unit test did, and it is a separate change."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T01:38:10.626578749Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id history-rust-seam
                title "Give the core a history interface a Rust caller can actually fill, because today only the C ABI can"
                priority 0
                kind @fix
                deps (history-translate)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-07T00:01:33.218350516Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "THE EDITOR HAS NO HISTORY FOR THE ONLY CONSUMER WE HAVE. hist_set is the sole way to attach one and it takes hist_fun_t, `unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void, *mut HistEventW, c_int, ...) -> c_int`. Stable Rust cannot define a variadic function, so the only values that can reach that slot are the history/history_w symbols nshedit-abi exports — and nsh links nshedit directly, not the ABI crate. Recall and search do nothing: ^P, ^N, k, j, ^R. fc is unaffected, because it reads the History object itself and never asks the editor.\n\nWHY IT SAT. history-idiomatize is P3 and deps on history-tests, which is also P3 and still todo, so the computed frontier never surfaced it. hist_set's own doc says \"idiomatization owes the core a history interface that is not a varargs dispatch\", which reads as a tidy-up rather than as the library failing at what it exists for. Nothing in the plan recorded the consumer requirement. This node is P0 and deps only on history-translate, deliberately: it must not sit behind a P3 test node.\n\nTHE SEAM IS MUCH SMALLER THAN THE VARARGS SIGNATURE SUGGESTS, measured rather than assumed. The hook is dispatched from exactly two places, both in hist.rs — hist_convert_str for the narrow store and hist_fun for the wide one. The editor issues exactly four operations through it: H_FIRST, H_LAST, H_NEXT, H_PREV, at hist.rs:577-592. All four pass a null trailing argument. The variadic-ness is unused by every call the editor makes; it exists only because the C ABI's history() happens to be variadic. And nshedit::history::History already exists as a native Rust type — it simply cannot be attached.\n\nSHAPE. ElHistoryT holds a source enum: the C-ABI (fun, ref) pair for nshedit-abi, or a boxed Rust hook for everyone else. The two dispatch sites match on it. hist_convert_str stays C-only, since a Rust consumer is never NARROW_HISTORY. Public surface is one trait with four methods returning the event number and the text, an EditLine setter, and an impl for History.\n\nOWNERSHIP: Box<dyn>, not a borrow. A borrow would put a lifetime parameter on EditLine, and EditLine is threaded through every signature in the crate — the cost lands everywhere, to buy something the caller can get back through an accessor. EditLine is already an owning aggregate; one more owned field is consistent with what it is."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-07T00:20:58.56732249Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "THE SAME HOLE EXISTS THREE TIMES, and dash — the first consumer to link nshedit directly rather than through the C ABI — found all three. The pattern is one thing, not three: the core's Rust API is complete for nshedit-abi and has gaps for a direct Rust caller, and two of the three gaps fail silently at runtime rather than failing to compile.\n\n1. el_set(EL_HIST) / hist_set. The subject of this node. Fixed by HistSource plus EditLine::set_history.\n\n2. el_init. It derives all three descriptors through a `fileno` stub that answers -1 unconditionally, because a FILE * is the C library's object and no-c-ffi.md reserves reaching into one for the ABI crate, which does it properly through cstdio::fileno_of. So an editor built through the core's el_init has stdin, stdout and stderr all at -1: every read is EBADF, el_wgets reports that as end of file (ERR-input-24), and the editor exits before a key is pressed. dash produced '\\r\\n' against the reference's '$ echo hi\\r\\nhi\\r\\n$ exit\\r\\n'. Not a bug in the stub — the bug is that el_init is public, looks like the entry point, and is silent. The doc even said \"a fileno of -1 is stored undiagnosed and construction still reports success\", which describes the C's weak reporting faithfully while here the -1 is unconditional. Now #[doc(hidden)] with the consequence spelled out, the way hist_set already was, so el_init_fd is the Rust entry point. dash on a pty in vi mode is now byte-identical to C dash against real libedit.\n\n3. el_gets. The core had only el_wgets, so every Rust consumer reimplements eln.c: encode wide to narrow through el_lgcyconv and rewrite *nread from a wide count to a byte count. Added to read.rs beside el_wgets. It copies the wide line out rather than reaching around the borrow with two raw pointers into one object as the C does — one allocation per line read, which is not where this library spends anything. The count and the string can disagree under EL_UNBUFFERED (ERR-core-api-26) and the doc says which to trust.\n\nWHAT TO DO WITH THE PATTERN. Auditing the rest of the core's `pub` surface for the same shape belongs in core-api-idiomatize: anything that is public, plausibly an entry point, and only correct when called from nshedit-abi. hist_set and el_init are now both hidden; the question is what else is. A doc-hidden item that a Rust caller cannot use correctly is the right answer, and the wrong one is leaving it visible with a doc comment describing the C.\n\nMEASURED WHILE WRITING THE TESTS, because both would be got wrong from intuition. OwnedHistoryW::new retains NOTHING until it is given a size — the store's max starts at 0 and history_def_enter trims to max on every insert, so an entry vanishes before the next call. with_size exists so the trap has a one-call answer. And H_ENTER returns 1 for a real insert and 0 when H_UNIQUE suppressed it, both \"success\", with -1 only for a NULL handle (ERR-history-25); the wrapper answers a bool for \"stored\" rather than passing that inversion on.\n\ned_prev_history has no CC_ERROR path at all. Walking past the oldest entry sets a flag, retries hist_get, discards the second answer (ERR-history-31) and returns CC_REFRESH_BEEP. The test expected CC_ERROR and was wrong."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-07T00:21:06.800956637Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-07T00:53:43.997681963Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "THE SEAM SHIPPED WITH TWO DESIGN ERRORS, both found by the consumer rather than by anything here, and both now fixed.\n\nOWNERSHIP WAS WRONG. set_history took the history by value, and the reasoning recorded for that — a borrow would put a lifetime parameter on EditLine, which is threaded through every signature in the crate — was about the cost to this crate and not about what the caller needs. The caller needs the two lifetimes to be independent, and that is verifiable rather than a preference: dash calls el_end at two sites while its history object survives, and history_end in a separate branch after the editor is already gone. `set +o emacs` is exactly that — end the editor, keep the history. The C says the same thing by making el_history.ref a client cookie el_end explicitly does not free, which was in the field's own doc comment the whole time.\n\nNow Rc<RefCell<dyn EditorHistory>>. That keeps EditLine free of a lifetime parameter, which was the real constraint, while making the editor a second holder rather than the owner. The RefCell is entered from exactly one place and never re-entered; a caller reaching back into its own history from inside one of these methods is the only way to panic, and that is documented at the variant.\n\nWIDTH WAS WRONG. HistLine carried Vec<u32> only. A shell's store is byte-oriented — dash's is HistoryGen<c_char>, which is what makes the C set NARROW_HISTORY and read ev.str as bytes — so a wide-only seam means the store transcodes on the way out and the editor transcodes back. HistLine.text is now HistText::Wide or HistText::Narrow, and hist_fun decodes the narrow arm through the same ct_decode_string the C's narrow path uses, so both agree on what an undecodable byte means.\n\nThat also moved the Rust check ahead of the NARROW_HISTORY split in hist_fun. It was after it, so a narrow editor with a Rust history fell into hist_convert_str, which can only reach a C dispatcher — the store would have been silently unreachable.\n\nTHE THIRD REPORTED CONCERN DOES NOT HOLD, and the measurement is worth keeping. The claim was that the opcode mapping must also cover H_CURR, H_SET, H_PREV_STR and H_NEXT_STR. Those constants exist, but nothing outside history.rs names any of them: grepping every H_* token in hist.rs, search.rs, common.rs, vi.rs and emacs.rs yields H_FIRST, H_LAST, H_NEXT, H_PREV, H_SETSIZE and H_SETUNIQUE and nothing else. The search commands walk through hist_first and hist_next like everything else. The four-method trait is total for what the editor issues.\n\nThe underlying worry was right even though the instance was not: an unmapped opcode made an editing command quietly do nothing. The catch-all arm was `_ => None`, indistinguishable from an empty history. It is now a debug_assert with the reasoning at the site, so if that measurement ever stops holding it fails loudly in a test build.\n\nTHE VARIADIC SLOT SURVIVES, as required — HistSource::CAbi is unchanged and a C caller passing its own hist_fun_t still works. All eight conformance stages pass, which is what says so."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id history-save-truncates
                title "A default build truncates the user's history file to a header and the next load calls it valid"
                priority 1
                kind @fix
                deps (history-listing-escape)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T01:47:24.130697479Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "SILENT DATA LOSS ON THE BUILD THAT SHIPS. Found by history-tests, pinned there in a_build_without_vis_cannot_write_the_legacy_format, and not fixed by that node because it is a defect rather than a missing test.\n\nThe sequence. history_save opens the file O_TRUNC. history_save_out then writes the 13-byte _HiStOrY_V2_ cookie, calls vis_encode for the first entry, gets None because the `bsd` feature is off, and returns -1. What is left on disk is the cookie and nothing else. The user's history is gone.\n\nThe second half is what makes it silent. history_load ACCEPTS that file — a valid cookie with no data lines is a legal empty history, not a corrupt one — so it reports zero entries and no error. Nothing anywhere says the file was destroyed. A user who runs a shell built this way loses their history and is told the file was empty.\n\nWHO IS AFFECTED. nshedit-abi pulls features = [\"bsd\"], so a C caller going through the ABI is fine and the conformance harness never sees this. A Rust program linking nshedit with default features is not fine — which is nsh, the reason this library exists.\n\nWHY IT SURVIVED. The vis stub is deliberate and documented: histfile.rs has a_legacy_file_is_unreadable_without_the_bsd_feature, and vis_encode's own comment says None must become the C's failure return \"never into unescaped output, which would be a wrong answer rather than a missing one\". That reasoning is right for the LISTING, where a failed escape means a line is not printed. It is wrong for SAVE, where the failure lands after the truncate and the cost is the file rather than the output.\n\nWHAT THE FIX PROBABLY IS. history-listing-escape just built vislite, a native strvis for VIS_NL with no bsd dependency. H_SAVE needs VIS_WHITE, which is VIS_SP|VIS_TAB|VIS_NL — the same encoder with three characters in the extra list instead of one. Extending vislite to take the flag set removes the None arm from the save path entirely and the failure mode with it.\n\nTwo things to get right whatever the fix. The truncate must not happen before the write can succeed — write to a temporary and rename, so a failure of any kind leaves the old file intact. And a cookie-only file should be distinguishable from a deliberately empty one, or the next occurrence of any write failure is equally silent.\n\nDo not close this by adding `bsd` to the default features. That would fix the symptom by making the default build depend on a sibling checkout, and it would leave the truncate-then-fail ordering in place for every other way a write can fail."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T01:57:53.809293856Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "FIXED BY REMOVING THE FAILURE, NOT BY HANDLING IT. vislite already implemented strvis for VIS_NL; VIS_WHITE is the same encoder with three characters in the extra list instead of one, so it became a parameter rather than a second implementation. history_save_out now calls it directly and vis_encode is gone — the write path has no way left to answer \"this build has no vis\", which is what made the truncate destructive.\n\nProven the same way the NL path was: the differential against bsd::vis over the whole corpus now runs both flag sets. VIS_WHITE matters more than VIS_NL did — a wrong escape in the listing costs a printed line, a wrong escape in the file is history that reloads as different text.\n\nThe regression test is deliberately not a round trip and deliberately not cfg'd. Reading still needs unvis, and the whole point is that writing no longer needs anything: saving_writes_the_entries_without_the_bsd_feature asserts the bytes on disk on a default build. Three save tests that were cfg'd on `bsd` are now unconditional for the same reason, and a_build_without_vis_cannot_write_the_legacy_format is deleted because it pinned behaviour that no longer exists.\n\nTWO THINGS THIS NODE DELIBERATELY DID NOT FIX, both because they are the C's and not ours.\n\nThe truncate ordering. history_save still opens O_TRUNC before it can know the write will succeed, so ENOSPC or EIO mid-file still leaves a short file. That is ERR-history-21 — the C ignores every write return and reports success — and it is registered as inherited. Writing to a temporary and renaming would fix it and is a real ABI divergence: it changes the inode, breaks a hardlinked history file, and changes what happens when the file is writable but its directory is not. That belongs in a divergence decision, not in a fix for a defect we introduced.\n\nhistory_load accepting a cookie-only file as a legal empty history. Also the C's, and correct on its own terms — a file with a valid cookie and no entries genuinely is an empty history. It only looked like part of this bug because our failure produced exactly that file. With the failure gone, the two are unrelated.\n\nWHAT THE SEAM IS NOW. crates/nshedit/src/histfile.rs's `bsd` block is one direction rather than two. Decoding stays there and stays gated, and the comment now says why that asymmetry is not an oversight: unvis has to accept every escape form vis can emit, including the VIS_CSTYLE and VIS_HTTPSTYLE ones nothing in this crate produces, so it is a decoder for the whole language rather than a subset of it. Encoding is a subset and vislite is that subset.\n\nAll eight conformance stages pass, which is what says the C ABI's save output is byte-identical to the oracle's."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T01:58:02.920782469Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

