            {
                id modes-idiomatize
                title "Idiomatize the editor command sets"
                deps (modes-tests)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:07:18.257444564Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Less than a line count would suggest, because the 96 commands really are thin — which was the expected honest answer. 5251 to 5141 lines, 215 to 214 functions. The full multiset of annotations across all six files is md5-identical before and after, so every one stayed attached to its subject.\n\nTHE ONE SHAPE SHARED FOURTEEN TIMES. Every motion command ends the same way: if a vi operator is armed, cv_delfini takes the span and the line is redrawn; otherwise only the cursor moved. Two spellings, differing solely in the C's `el_map.type == MAP_VI` conjunct. Now end_motion and end_vi_motion, applied at thirteen sites across all three files. The two are kept apart deliberately, with a doc saying which motions the C gave the keymap test to and which it did not, BECAUSE THAT SPLIT IS NOT PRINCIPLED and a reader should see it at the call site rather than discover it.\n\nTWO \"BELONGS ELSEWHERE\" NOTES THAT WERE ALREADY STALE. emacs.rs and common.rs each carried private MODE_INSERT/MODE_REPLACE/MODE_REPLACE_1 with comments saying chared \"does not export the MODE_* constants yet\". It does, and vi.rs already imported them. Both now import.\n\nkill_save and kill_copy were the same C loop twice, one per file, with the emacs copy unbounded and the common copy clamped. Merged, with the doc that says the clamp is dead — as both docs already argued independently.\n\nMY BRIEF WAS WRONG ABOUT sem:vi.vi-match-fn AND THE AGENT CAUGHT IT. I said the rule describes the cursor advance as conditional and needs correcting. It was already corrected, by 15b61ce, which rewrote step 6 to say the advance is unconditional and amended step 3 to record that the size_t delta is SIZE_MAX in the backward case; ERR-modes-73 carries the disposition. The stale party was vi.rs's own comment, which asserted a disagreement between code and rule that no longer existed. Comment fixed; no rule touched, no bump. Correcting a rule that was already correct would have been the worse outcome.\n\nALSO WRONG IN MY BRIEF: common.rs does not contain one of the five wide-slice-versus-ASCII helpers. They are in el.rs, hist.rs, map.rs, parse.rs and terminal.rs — none in this concern.\n\nWHERE THE EXTRACTED HELPERS ACTUALLY BELONG, all cross-concern. end_motion and end_vi_motion belong in chared.rs beside cv_delfini, which they call and whose c_vcmd they read; they sit in vi.rs and are imported by the other two. kill_save belongs in chared.rs beside cv_yank, which writes the same buffer under different bounds discipline. ARGUMENT_CAP is a property of ElState.argument and belongs in el.rs. common.rs's private iswdigit belongs in locale.rs beside iswspace, as its own doc says. And common.rs's hist_save_line/hist_saved_line reimplement the live-line stash hist.rs owns.\n\nSIX TEST MODULES CARRY BYTE-IDENTICAL text(el) AND killed(el). vi, common, emacs (this concern) plus chared, hist and search. testkit.rs is the obvious home and already holds headless_editor and set_line; left alone rather than edit a just-landed shared file under three concurrent agents. That is the fourth thing testkit should absorb.\n\nFIVE THINGS DELIBERATELY LEFT C-SHAPED, each with a reason that is not laziness. `let _ = c;` in about 55 commands would be `_c: u32` in Rust — but that leaves roughly fifteen of them as a bare single delegating call, which is trivial-function's exact description, and the discard is where several carry the C's /*ARGSUSED*/ provenance. vi_histedit's 110 procedural lines are annotated against numbered rule steps and five errata ids; splitting scatters the citations across helpers with no independent meaning. ed_insert's `loop { let more = count != 0; count = count.wrapping_sub(1); }` is ugly and is exactly the post-decrement asymmetry ERR-modes-75 freezes — a for loop would not reproduce it. The cv_undo / el_map.current pairs in eleven vi commands look extractable but their ORDER differs per command and decides the cursor offset the snapshot records. And ed_prev_line/ed_next_line's signed scans hide an in-place argument decrement that ERR-modes-02 and -20 pin."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:07:33.617601976Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
