                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:41:53.89815562Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Two traps in blank_editline that any later test author building on it will hit, both worked around in this file's el_with with the reason in its doc comment.\n\nblank_editline leaves el_state.lastcmd and el_state.thiscmd both zero, and fn_complete2 reads equal values as the SECOND consecutive invocation of the same command. A test built straight on it therefore takes the `?` listing path instead of the first-tab path, silently — it completes, it just completes the wrong way. el_with moves thiscmd off lastcmd.\n\nblank_editline also leaves el_outfd at 0. Both write_outfile and the beep take that literally, so an unmodified blank editor writes BEL to the process's stdin. el_with sets it to -1, and the listing tests hand it a real file.\n\nThree expectations written from reading rather than running turned out wrong.\n\nThe listing gap is two spaces, not one. fn_display_match_list's column separator (`col != 0 ? \" \" : \"\"`) is written ON TOP OF the append character rather than instead of it, so three-character names at width 3 come out \"abc  abd \". The trailing space on the last column is the append character with nothing following it, which is why every listed line ends in whitespace.\n\n`'\\''` in the tokenizer does not do what it looks like. Inside single quotes the backslash is emitted literally, the next quote closes, and the fourth reopens — so the string is an unmatched quote and returns 1. The real property is `'a\\'` giving [\"a\\\\\"]: a backslash does not defend the closing quote inside single quotes.\n\n`gen` is a reserved keyword in edition 2024. Cost a compile round, nothing more.\n\ntokenizer.fun-tok-end-fn is left without a unit test of its own on purpose: it is drop(tok), and there is nothing to assert that the type system does not already guarantee. It stays verified by hist_tok.c."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id completion-idiomatize
                title "Idiomatize completion and tokenization"
                deps (completion-tests)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:29:28.635412323Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "filecomplete.rs 19 to 22 production functions, tokenizer.rs flat. No exported signature moved — the whole surface was checked, and everything retyped is static in the C and private here.\n\nTHE TRAP WAS THE SIZE-COUNTING PASS, and it is gone. escape_filename asks the same question twice, in two differently-shaped conditionals: once to count how many bytes the result needs and once to emit them. They must agree byte for byte or the buffer is the wrong size, and nothing made them agree except care. Both now go through one escaped_byte classifier, so the counting pass sums 0/1/3 off the same value the emitting pass matches on. Equivalence was verified context by context before collapsing — Bare gives +1 only when escaping is needed, Single gives +3 for a quote, Double gives +1 for a double-quote-needing byte, using the fact that the double-quote set is a subset of the general one.\n\nTHE TWO MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE FLAGS BECAME UNREPRESENTABLE RATHER THAN MERELY TRUE. sem:filecomplete.escape-filename-fn step 2 asserts the two bools cannot both be set; a Quoting enum says it in the type. Both ERR-completion-08 and -09 stay on the arms that carry them.\n\nwhat_to_do(el) NAMES THE lastcmd == thiscmd PROTOCOL. Its doc says outright that an editor whose two command slots agree takes the listing path on its FIRST invocation, which is the trap a fresh blank_editline() used to spring silently — the completion tests took the `?` path instead of the first-tab path and still passed. It belongs on EditLine as is_repeat_of_last_command so every reader of those two fields meets the protocol, not just this module; el.rs is another concern's, so it is a free function here.\n\ncompletion_matches's accumulator stopped being an Option<Vec>, which removed an `as_mut()?` early return that silently meant \"no matches\". And try_reserve_exact is now passed the argument's actual meaning — the shortfall against the requested length, not against the capacity.\n\ntokenizer: the flags word became two bools, so ten bit-twiddles are gone and every arm says which bit it means. wmax and amax deleted — both were exactly the slice lengths on every path, and they exist in the C only because a char* cannot report its own end, so removing them removes an invariant that could only ever be broken. Field visibility narrowed to the two slots the ABI and parse.rs actually resolve against, which is what the C's opaque handle already promised.\n\nONE RULE THAT COULD LICENSE A BETTER SHAPE, not changed. sem:filecomplete.find-word-to-complete-fn's ownership paragraph says NULL is returned only on allocation failure and *length has already been written by then, so a caller must not assume it is untouched. That sentence is the only thing keeping the &mut usize out-parameter: a Rust Option<(Vec<u32>, usize)> makes the length unobservable on the failure path and so contradicts it. Nothing in tree depends on it — fn_complete2 initialises len to 0 and returns without reading it. Relaxing the rule to \"unspecified on the failure path\" would let the out-parameter go. Deliberate bump, so both were left alone.\n\nTWO THINGS DECLINED FOR REASONS WORTH KEEPING. completion_matches's manual capacity doubling stays, because it is the ONLY place the function can produce the C's allocation-failure None — which step 2 and ERR-completion-06 both describe as an observable outcome indistinguishable from \"no matches\". A Vec that grows itself deletes that return value. And QuoteT's variant names stay QNone through QDoubleone rather than Rust's None/Single/DoubleOne, because every state table in docs/spec/port/src/tokenizer.md names them that way and is read directly against these arms; renaming breaks the correspondence for a cosmetic gain.\n\nThe ten narrow/wide instantiation wrappers at the foot of tokenizer.rs are each one delegating call — exactly trivial-function's shape — but they are the C's two compilation units and their def rules live in histedit.md. Left, and none added."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:29:36.110990454Z
