                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T18:30:29.574929547Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "REOPENING. I completed this node earlier today on insufficient evidence, and the first run of the new el_api conformance driver disproved it.\n\nWhat I checked: `grep -rn 'todo!\\|unimplemented!'` over every crate, which returned one site in search.rs, plus the wave-2 gate reading 650/650. Both were true and neither was sufficient.\n\nWhat the driver measured: 22 of 88 operations diverge, and the shape says one cause rather than twenty. Every el_set and el_get returns -1 on the port where the oracle returns 0 — except `EL_EDITOR bogus`, which both reject, so the entry point is reached and the argument is seen.\n\n    0003 EL_EDITOR emacs      oracle rc=0    port rc=-1\n    0007 EL_EDITOR bogus      oracle rc=-1   port rc=-1\n    0009 EL_SIGNAL 1          oracle rc=0    port rc=-1\n    0022 EL_GET terminal      oracle rc=0 <dumb>   port rc=-1 <(null)>\n\nThe cause is written down in the code, at crates/nshedit-abi/src/eln.rs:434: \"Step 2 and the whole dispatch are unwritten, so every op takes the C's default arm.\" The comment then specifies, in detail and correctly, what each arm owes — it is a deliberate, well-documented stub awaiting the varargs work, not an oversight.\n\nWhy no gate caught it. It has a body, it returns a value, and the value is right for an unsupported op, so stub-scan does not fire. The wave-2 gate measures whether an ANNOTATION is present, and `[spec:libedit:def:eln.el-set-fn]` sits on the function — so 650/650 counts a stub as translated. That is the gap this whole wave-3 exercise exists to close, and it is worth stating that the gate cannot close it on its own: only executing the code does.\n\nTwo things follow.\n\nThe narrow el_set/el_get dispatch is the remaining work on this node, and abi-varargs is what unblocks it — the arms read their arguments with `ap.next_arg::<T>()`, which is why they were left until c_variadic landed. It has landed.\n\nMore generally: completing a translate node needs evidence that the code RUNS, not that it exists. The six siblings I closed alongside this one were closed on the same reasoning and deserve the same suspicion. conformance-driver-readline and conformance-driver-ub are the instruments for that, and this is now a reason to build them rather than a nice-to-have."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id core-api-tests
                title "Write tests for the EditLine lifecycle and public API from their sem rules; there are none to port"
                deps (
                    core-api-translate
                    abi-callback-types
                    abi-visibility
                    abi-varargs
                    abi-narrow
                    platform-build
                )
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:35:40.475369128Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:36:03.151628913Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Four things this pass found that are not test code, kept together because three of them are documents that have drifted behind the code and one is a real gap.\n\nA REAL FUNCTIONAL GAP IN THE NARROW el_get. There is no arm for EL_EDITOR, EL_WORDCHARS, EL_PROMPT, EL_RPROMPT, EL_PROMPT_ESC or EL_RPROMPT_ESC, so el_get answers -1 and writes nothing where the C answers 0 and writes a value. This is a divergence we introduced, not a defect of the C we reproduced, and the setter side handles the same ops — abi-elset-dispatch built the decode arm for el_set and stopped. Needs its own node under abi-surface; the test written for it documents the current answer with a comment saying so rather than asserting the C's.\n\nTHE eln.rs MODULE HEADER IS FALSE. It says el_set and el_get \"dispatch on no op ... every op falls to the C's default arm and comes back -1\". That was true before abi-elset-dispatch and has not been true since: el_set_va has the forwarded set, the five list ops, EL_HIST, the prompt family and the EL_EDITOR/EL_WORDCHARS decode, and el_get_va forwards nine. A reader trusting the header would conclude the narrow API is a stub.\n\ndocs/errata.md CARRIES THE SAME DRIFT. ERR-core-api-09 still reads \"open — not carried out ... el_set returns -1 for every op\", but the NULL rejections it prescribes are implemented and are now asserted. ERR-core-api-29 still reads \"the narrow el_get's half is still a comment only — that dispatch is a blanket -1 stub\", but EL_GETTC is in FORWARDED_TO_WGET and reads `li` back. Both statuses are wrong about the tree.\n\nsem:histedit.el-beep-fn MISDESCRIBES THE C. It says the terminal layer emits a visible-bell capability, otherwise the audible bell string, and writes nothing if neither is available. src/terminal.c:864 tests only T_bl and writes a literal 0x07 otherwise: there is no visible-bell branch and it never writes nothing. The port follows the C; the rule is wrong. Left uncorrected for now because five other test nodes were mid-flight against the current spec and a rule-version bump cascades their nodes to stale. It belongs with sem:tty.tty-printchar-fn, which terminal-tests found wrong the same way, in one spec-correction pass.\n\nTwo measurements that had to be made rather than reasoned about. EL_SETFP cannot be handed a fabricated FILE *: el_init_fd stores the three streams verbatim without touching them, but el_wset routes through cstdio::fileno_of, which calls the real fileno(3) and dereferences. The only testable argument is NULL; distinct sentinel streams have to go in through the constructor and come back through EL_GETFP. And EL_SAFEREAD answers 256 — the raw FIXIO bit, not a boolean; EL_SIGNAL only looks like it round-trips because HANDLE_SIGNALS is 0x001."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id core-api-idiomatize
                title "Idiomatize the EditLine lifecycle and public API"
                deps (core-api-tests)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T09:01:56.204420688Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T09:45:23.387605916Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "THE CROSS-CUTTING GROUNDWORK, done first because all three pieces touch types every concern uses and so cannot run in parallel with the other eight nodes. Net -153 lines. No sem rule described any of the fifteen functions — checked, and `git diff -U0 | grep spec:libedit` on the whole change is empty — so nothing moved, nothing bumped.\n\nONE OUTPUT SINK. Fifteen definitions across eight files became three in a new `stdio.rs`, 88 call sites rewritten. It sits beside `errno` and `locale` under lib.rs's existing \"host facilities the port has to supply itself\" section, whose comment already cited hoisting duplicated copies as its reason — errno twice, the locale layer twice, and now the writers fifteen times. All fifteen were behaviourally identical: same fd < 0 guard, same ManuallyDrop, same write_all. The only difference was the return type, and only terminal.rs reads it, so the consolidated pair keeps terminal.rs's i32.\n\nTHE trivial-function GATE WAS RESOLVED STRUCTURALLY RATHER THAN BYPASSED, and how is worth keeping. A free `write_outfile(el, b) -> write_fd(el.el_outfd, b)` trips it, because `el.el_outfd` is a projection of a parameter and the call binds no information. The same body rooted at `self` does not — the detector's own comment says a self-rooted body binds encapsulated state. So they are methods on EditLine. Five trivial sites remain in the crate and all five predate this change.\n\nONE SHARED TEST EDITOR, and it absorbed a sixth. testkit::headless_editor runs el_init_internal's subsystem sequence in the C's own documented order, each init carrying a comment saying what breaks without it, with terminal_init/tty_init/sig_init excluded for stated reasons and the screen they would have produced filled in instead. No flags and no builder: the only parameter is screen size, which is honest variation — refresh/test.rs needs 8x3 because every wrap, margin and rotation case is reached by running off a row.\n\nRemoving the omissions changed what the tests were running against. vi/test.rs had been hand-setting MAP_VI, current = Alt and wordchars; map_init installs all three. Eleven redundant read_init calls are gone. And one hist test broke correctly: it had been RELYING on the helper's missing hist_init, with its doc comment saying so, and now stages the failed-allocation state explicitly. Seven chared tests still build on bare blank_editline deliberately, because they are testing ch_init itself — the uninitialised editor is the subject, not the fixture.\n\nThe state those helpers ran in — empty keymaps, wordchars None — is one el_init cannot produce. No outcome changed when it was corrected, but the bug class was live in two more places than the three already known.\n\nFOUR THINGS THE EIGHT QUEUED NODES WILL HIT.\n\nA fresh worktree cannot run cargo test --workspace at all. git worktree add gives every file a new mtime, so make decides aclocal.m4 is stale and reaches for aclocal-1.18, which is not installed; all eight conformance tests fail before a line of Rust is touched. The fix is to touch the autotools chain in dependency order — configure.ac, acinclude.m4, m4/*.m4, then aclocal.m4, then configure and config.h.in, then */Makefile.in — and remove target/conformance/oracle-build. And cargo build must run before cargo test --workspace or six tests fail on a missing libnshedit.so. Both belong in worktree setup rather than being rediscovered eight times.\n\ntrivial-function is a STAGED-DIFF gate, so nplan check does not show it and the commit is the first thing that does.\n\nmap_init ends by calling map_init_vi, so the shipped default after init is vi INSERT mode. Anything assuming an emacs default after map_init is wrong.\n\nElMapCurrent already derived Clone, Copy, PartialEq and Eq — only Debug was missing. Twenty-five assert!(a == b) and twenty tuple-shaped CoordT comparisons are now assert_eq!, and positions read CoordT { h, v }, which names the fields — relevant because t_size stores columns in v."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:24:11.503450238Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The concern's own work, after the cross-cutting groundwork. el_source's name resolution extracted to editrc_path, so a 148-line function is a one-line call plus the read loop; auxv_at_secure walks chunks_exact; three doc comments corrected to state facts rather than give advice.\n\nONE AUDIT ACTION TAKEN. NARROW_HISTORY is now #[doc(hidden)]. It is the only el_flags bit that is pub at all, it is public solely so eln.rs can raise it, and a Rust caller raising it makes hist_command's `history size` and `history unique` answer -1 for a store that would have handled them — because the bit is tested before the HistSource::Rust arm.\n\nONE REFACTOR TRIED AND REVERTED, recorded at the site: hoisting locale::charset() out of el_source's per-line loop. The C's iswspace reads LC_CTYPE per call and our snapshot is replaceable, so hoisting trades a faithful property for a thread-local read per line.\n\nTHE PUBLIC-SURFACE AUDIT, which was the point of the node. Five items match the pattern — public, entry-point-shaped, only correct from nshedit-abi: el_init and mb_cur_max (already hidden), NARROW_HISTORY (now hidden), and el_lgcylinfo plus the three CFile stream fields, which cannot be hidden without contradicting def:el.editline's member list and so are documented instead. Everything else public is genuinely Rust-usable.\n\nTHE INVERSE OF THE PATTERN IS THE REAL FINDING, and it was not acted on. Seven of the eight el_flags bits are pub(crate) and nothing public sets them, so a direct Rust consumer cannot reach those el_set ops at all. Concretely: SIGNAL HANDLING IS UNREACHABLE FROM RUST — read_prepare and read_finish gate sig_set/sig_clr on HANDLE_SIGNALS and nothing public sets it. Same for EL_UNBUFFERED and EL_SAFEREAD. EL_EDITMODE off is reachable only through parse_line or an .editrc. Everything else el_wset does already has a public core function or field, so the flag word is the whole of the remaining gap — the same three-holes shape this node opened with. Closing it means new public API or making the bits pub, and the second makes the surface more C-shaped rather than less, so it needs a decision rather than a patch.\n\nRECOMMENDATION ON el_init: make it pub(crate) and drop the doc-hidden with it. Hiding removes it from rustdoc and leaves it callable, occupying the name a Rust caller reaches for first — and every editor it builds is dead on arrival with three descriptors at -1. Nothing in the tree calls it: the exported el_init in the ABI calls el_init_fd with cstdio::fileno_of, and the only other caller is its own test. pub(crate) costs nothing and keeps the rules annotated where they are. Deleting it would be wrong — sem:el.el-init-fn's tail call has to have a counterpart. Left for a decision because it is a public-API removal.\n\nRECOMMENDATION ON THE NARROW el_get GAP: close it as one node in two pieces of different weight. EL_EDITOR and EL_WORDCHARS are a pure omission — el_wget already answers them and no errata entry licenses -1; ERR-core-api-30 presumes the arm exists. The four prompt ops are ERR-core-api-18's reproduce disposition, still open, and the arm must store the truncated char unconditionally including the NUL from a failed prompt_get. Both change what eln/tests.rs pins, so both need the bump decision.\n\nERRATA WORDING, corrected but not applied — the register is not this node's to edit. ERR-core-api-09 should move from open to defined: the narrow setter rejects a NULL argument before decoding and rejects an undecodable string, so neither reaches wcscmp or wcsdup. ERR-core-api-29 should move from partial to reproduced: EL_GETTC is in eln.rs's FORWARDED_TO_WGET and the variadic tail is handed straight on, which is the whole of what the op needs. Two more are stale from the same cause: ERR-core-api-31's status ends \"Vacuously so for now, since every op returns -1\", which is false, and the summary block's prose says eln.rs \"is a blanket -1 stub ... because Rust cannot yet define a C-variadic function on stable\" — it is not a stub and it does define them. If -09 and -29 move, the table goes reproduced 201->202, defined 118->119, partial 33->32, open 7->6.\n\nTWO CHANGES TO SHARED TYPES WANTED AND NOT MADE. impl Drop for EditLine is the one that matters: there is none, so a Rust caller who lets a Box<EditLine> fall out of scope never runs el_end, tty_cookedmode is never called, and the terminal is left in raw mode after the program exits. el_end takes the box by value precisely so a second teardown cannot be written, which makes the two designs mutually exclusive — the only shape that works is Drop running the forward sequence with el_end reduced to consuming the box. sem:el.el-end-fn reasons explicitly about why the sequence is calls rather than Drop, and that survives, but \"an editor that is never ended leaves the terminal alone\" stops being true. And #[derive(Default)] on CoordT, purely additive.\n\nCROSS-CUTTING DUPLICATION VISIBLE FROM HERE, same shape as the fifteen output sinks: five near-identical wide-slice-versus-ASCII-literal helpers (el.rs, hist.rs, map.rs, parse.rs, terminal.rs), and eight literal copies of the zeroed CtBufferT across both crates, which a CtBufferT::new() in chartype.rs collapses."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T10:24:21.004245204Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        sealed true
    }

