            {
                id readline-compat-tests
                title "Write tests for the readline compatibility surface from its sem rules; there are none to port"
                deps (
                    readline-compat-translate
                    abi-callback-types
                    abi-visibility
                    abi-varargs
                    abi-narrow
                    abi-errno
                    platform-build
                )
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T23:46:04.465295858Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "SCOPE CORRECTION. This node's tests went to crates/nshedit-abi/src/readline.rs, not crates/nshedit/src/editline.rs. The latter holds only the header typedefs (sem:readline.rl-*-t); every readline.*-fn and every exported global lives in the ABI crate, which is the actual port of src/readline.c. Both match the crates/**/*.rs glob the port gate reads, so the claims count from either.\n\nFIVE RULES LEFT UNCOVERED, and the reasons are not interchangeable.\n\nrl-getc-function-fn: its load-bearing claim is that rl_initialize samples the hook once for non-NULL-ness and setting it afterwards has no effect. Verifying that means running rl_initialize, which builds an editor on the real fd 0. Not worth wedging a terminal for one rule. The adapter's read-at-call-time behaviour is covered under getc-function-fn.\n\nrl-prep-terminal-fn and rl-deprep-terminal-fn: each is one el_set(EL_PREP_TERM, n) landing in tty_rawmode/tty_cookedmode. On a pipe tcgetattr fails at step 3 and no field changes, so there is nothing to assert. Needs a pty.\n\nrl-resize-terminal-fn: el_resize only rebuilds when terminal_get_size reports a change, and that comparison is against capability values seeded from TIOCGWINSZ on el_infd. On a pipe there is no window size, so the call is provably a no-op — measured by corrupting t_size to (1,1) and watching it stay (1,1).\n\nrl-echo-signal-char-fn: blocked by a core gap rather than by the environment. tty_get_signal_character is pub(crate) and the ABI crate cannot reach it, so readline.rs stands it in with a hard -1 and the function always takes its early return. re_putc is a no-op stand-in too. Nothing is observable until the core publishes both.\n\nFOUR EXPECTATIONS THAT WERE WRONG, which is the useful part.\n\nrl_reset_terminal does not discard pending pushback. el_reset calls ch_reset, and ch_reset never touches the macro queue — chared.rs:876 says so explicitly. A key pushed before the reset is still delivered after it.\n\nreadline() turns one CRLF line into two. noedit_wgets breaks on \\r OR \\n, and readline()'s trim only removes \\n, so the \\r survives in the line and the orphaned \\n comes back as a following empty string. A program reading a DOS-line-ended file through readline() sees twice as many lines as the file has, every other one blank. Faithful to the C.\n\nrl_read_key can never read a non-terminal descriptor: el_wgetc step 3 is `if tty_rawmode(el) < 0 { return 0 }`, and tty_rawmode fails on anything tcgetattr rejects, reported as end of file (ERR-input-24). Only el_wgets's NO_TTY branch bypasses el_wgetc and reaches the descriptor, which is exactly why readline() works over a pipe here and rl_read_key does not.\n\nThe pushback queue is FIFO, not the C's ma->level stack: el_wgetc step 2 always takes macro[0], the oldest.\n\nTWO THINGS FOR SOMEONE'S ATTENTION, neither a behavioural defect. crates/nshedit-abi/src/lib.rs still says \"until then every function is todo!(), so its parameters are unread\" and readline.rs's module docs still list the narrow el_set/el_get dispatch as an open blocker. There are zero todo!() bodies in the crate and the dispatch works — two of these tests depend on it. Same drift core-api-tests found in eln.rs. And _rl_qsort_string_compare clamps to -1/0/1 where the C returns strcoll's raw value; only the sign is contractual and nothing in libedit calls it, but a differential driver comparing return values rather than signs would disagree.\n\n_el_rl_tstp turned out testable after all: nshedit-plat's install_handler plus set_signal_slot let the test take SIGTSTP over, observe the raise through the trampoline's slot, and restore the disposition before asserting. set_signal_ops was deliberately not used — it is a one-way seam with no way to take it back off, which would poison every later test in the process."
                    }

                    {
