                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:59:20.9116563Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "5094 to 5039 lines, 141 to 144 functions, 153 exported symbols unchanged, 167 annotations unchanged. The rejected list is the deliverable here as much as the changes, because dec:libedit:idiomatic-care freezes the C shape in this crate on purpose and most of what looks refactorable is the ABI.\n\nWHAT WAS DONE. Three stale docs, and they mattered: the module docs and the extern block comment both still listed \"the narrow el_set/el_get dispatch is unwritten, still dispatch on no op\" as an open blocker, which has been false since abi-elset-dispatch and which two of this file's own tests disprove. append_history's comment block contradicted itself across two paragraphs about whether a NULL cookie goes over. Then: the path-resolution triplicated across read_history, write_history and history_file_path became one history_file_name; twenty-one spelled-out zeroed HistEvent literals became one EMPTY_EVENT const whose doc records why the port zeroes what the C leaves uninitialised; two C-array walks and three free-what-was-collected loops got named helpers; and read_one_byte's private WOULD_BLOCK == -2 sentinel became io::Result, with the C-facing return unchanged.\n\nTWELVE CANDIDATES REJECTED, and the reasons are the useful part. rl_bind_key's retval accumulator is narrated as literal steps by its own rule, so early returns would put code out of step-for-step correspondence for no behavioural gain. strcmp_pointer_repr's byte loop IS ERR-readline-01 rather than a helper around it — an iterator form would hide the defect the function exists to exhibit. strchr and strchr_nonul differ precisely in whether the terminator matches, load-bearing at both call sites. readline()'s loop-with-break is the setjmp emulation and continue is the longjmp. history_search and history_search_pos look alike and have different directions, returns, rules and errata. And _rl_completion_append_character_function versus append_char_str are not two renderings of one global: the first is the C's shared-char[2] face with its own rule and test, the second is the shape filecomplete's AppFunc takes.\n\nONE CHANGE TOUCHED CODE UNDER AN UNTESTED RULE, and it was declared. rl_initialize is covered by rl-getc-function-fn, whose claim — the hook is sampled once for non-NULL-ness — has no oracle. Its HistEvent literal became EMPTY_EVENT; the const is field-for-field identical, the ev it declares is used only for H_SETSIZE, and the sampling block itself is byte-for-byte untouched. The other four untested rules' functions were not touched at all.\n\nA RULE WHOSE STATED REASON IS INVERTED. sem:readline.rl-get-previous-history-fn says the C's `while (count--)` means \"a count of 0 or negative pushes nothing, because the test is a post-decrement\". That is backwards: with count == -1 the controlling expression is -1, which is TRUE, so the C pushes and keeps decrementing until signed overflow. Only count == 0 pushes nothing. The port implements what the rule PRESCRIBES — nothing for <= 0 — which is the safe reading, so no code is wrong; but the justification does not hold and the negative-count case is unregistered UB in the C. Contrast sem:readline.rl-insert-fn, which describes its `for (; count > 0; count--)` correctly.\n\nA CORRECTION TO MY OWN BRIEF. I said readline.rs reimplements ct_enc_width as a private enc_width. It does not — that stand-in is in eln.rs:105 with wctob and sum_enc_widths beside it, out of this node's scope. The underlying gap is real and located elsewhere: nshedit::chartype::ct_enc_width is pub(crate).\n\nFIVE CORE ITEMS STILL pub(crate) AND WHAT EACH COSTS. tty_get_signal_character is the one that matters: it is why rl_echo_signal_char has a hard -1 stand-in and always takes its early return, and publishing it in nshedit::tty is what closes that rule — the only one of the five blocked by code rather than by needing a terminal. Then tty_init, tty_end, re_putc, em_kill_line and el_init_internal, which keep five stand-ins and the NO_RESET gap alive. And both crate::stdio and crate::vislite are pub(crate), so this crate cannot reach either even where it would want to — though rl_out_write specifically must NOT use stdio, because stdio writes the editor's el_outfd while rl_out_write must write the application's rl_outstream."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:59:31.412543707Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
