            {
                id abi-soname
                title "Give the cdylib a SONAME and the install symlinks a consumer's DT_NEEDED actually records"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T15:38:17.60379371Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "DECIDED: SONAME libnshedit.so.0, with libedit.so.2 as an install symlink onto it. Do NOT claim libreadline.so.8 yet.\n\nMeasured: Debian's libedit.so.2 carries SONAME libedit.so.2, libreadline.so.8 carries libreadline.so.8, and our cdylib carries NONE. A library's SONAME is copied into every program that links it, as DT_NEEDED, and the loader then looks for a file with exactly that name. Ours having none means anything linked against it records the path it was handed rather than a stable name, so moving or renaming the file breaks the binary.\n\nWhy our own name rather than claiming libedit.so.2 outright: a newly linked program records what we actually are, which is honest and debuggable -- ldd names us. Every binary already installed has DT_NEEDED libedit.so.2 baked in, and the symlink is what carries those. Both consumers are served and only one of them is told a story about what it loaded.\n\nWhy NOT libreadline.so.8, and this is the one worth holding: we export 149 of its 736 symbols, measured. A program needing any of the other 587 fails at LOAD time with an unresolved symbol, which is a worse outcome than us simply not being installed -- the program would have worked with real readline and now does not start at all. The readline surface is a compatibility layer over libedit's own, not a reimplementation of GNU readline, and the symbol count says so. Revisit only if the count gets close, and even then the failure mode argues for opt-in rather than a symlink a distribution drops in by default.\n\nNote the numbers are Debian's libedit at 3.1-20250104 (soname .so.2, libtool 0.75) while our source is 20260512-3.1 at LT_VERSION 0:78:0. If we ever bump our own soname, that is our version and not upstream's, which is another reason not to adopt theirs as ours."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T15:54:30.534990253Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "DECIDED: SONAME libnshedit.so.0, with libedit.so.2 installed as a symlink onto it. Do NOT claim libreadline.so.8 yet.\n\nMeasured on this host:\n\n    /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libedit.so.2       SONAME libedit.so.2\n    /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libreadline.so.8   SONAME libreadline.so.8\n    target/debug/libnshedit.so                   SONAME (none)\n\nWhy a SONAME at all: the linker copies a library's SONAME into the consuming program's DT_NEEDED, and the loader then looks for a file with exactly that name. With none, anything newly linked against us records the path it was handed instead of a stable name, so moving or renaming the file breaks the binary. That is a defect regardless of what name we choose.\n\nWhy our own name rather than claiming libedit.so.2 directly: a program linked fresh against us should record what it actually depends on. The symlink is what serves the existing population -- every binary already installed carries DT_NEEDED libedit.so.2, so a file of that name has to be us for them to load us at all, and a symlink does that without us impersonating a version we do not own.\n\nWHY NOT libreadline.so.8, and this is the load-bearing half: we export 149 of its 736 symbols. Measured, not estimated -- and note the port is not behind libedit here, since Debian's libedit.so.2 exports 148 of the same set and the oracle 149. The problem is the denominator. A program that needs any of the other 587 and finds a libreadline.so.8 that is us fails at load with an unresolved symbol, which is a WORSE outcome than us not being installed: without the symlink it gets real readline and works. Claiming the soname converts \"not installed\" into \"installed and broken\". Revisit only with a measurement of what real consumers actually reference, not with a symbol count.\n\nFollow-on for whoever builds this: cdylib SONAME is set through the linker, so this is a rustflags or build-script concern rather than a Cargo.toml field, and it needs to survive both debug and release profiles. Verify with `objdump -p ... | grep SONAME` and add that assertion to conformance/abi-shape.sh, which currently checks symbol names and would not notice a missing or wrong SONAME at all."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T14:06:57.006461209Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T14:08:34.911095447Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "AMENDS the compat symlink set on this node: it must be BOTH libedit.so.2 and libedit.so.0, not libedit.so.2 alone. The earlier entry measured only Debian's binary and read its soname as libedit's. It is not.\n\nMeasured, at the source rather than the artifact:\n\n    this tree,      configure.ac:38      LT_VERSION 0:75:0 -> 0:78:0\n    oracle build,   readelf -d           SONAME libedit.so.0, file libedit.so.0.0.78\n    Debian 3.1-20250104                  SONAME libedit.so.2, file libedit.so.2.0.75\n    debian/patches/update-soname.diff    -AC_SUBST(LT_VERSION, [0:75:0])\n                                         +AC_SUBST(LT_VERSION, [2:75:0])\n\nSo libedit.so.2 is a Debian patch, one line of it, and libedit's own soname is and always has been libedit.so.0. libtool reads current:revision:age and names the object lib.so.(current-age), which is how 2:75:0 becomes .so.2.0.75 and 0:78:0 becomes .so.0.0.78.\n\nThe consequence is that a compat set of {libedit.so.2} serves Debian and Ubuntu and nothing else. Fedora, Arch, the BSDs, and anyone who ran ./configure themselves all have DT_NEEDED libedit.so.0 baked into their binaries — including, note, the oracle drivers this repo's own harness builds. Shipping only .so.2 would have left the most common case, a from-source build, unable to load us at all.\n\nBoth symlinks point at libnshedit.so.0, so this costs one more line and no new decision about what we are. The reasoning of the original entry is untouched: our own SONAME stays libnshedit.so.0 so a newly linked program records what it actually loaded, the compat names exist only to carry binaries that were linked before we existed, and libreadline.so.8 is still refused at 149 of 736 symbols because an unresolved-symbol failure at LOAD time is worse than not being installed.\n\nWhat this does introduce is that we now claim two filenames a distribution's own package manager also claims, rather than one. Recorded in packaging/install.sh, which prints every symlink it creates and takes --no-compat to skip them."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T14:15:41.566972783Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id abi-elset-dispatch
                title "Write the narrow el_set/el_get dispatch, which is a stub returning -1 for every op"
                priority 0
                kind @feat
                deps (abi-varargs)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-06T18:39:05.244741977Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Measured by conformance/driver/el_api.c on its first run: 22 of 88 operations diverge, and every one is an el_set or el_get returning -1 where the oracle returns 0.\n\n    0003 EL_EDITOR emacs   oracle rc=0          port rc=-1\n    0007 EL_EDITOR bogus   oracle rc=-1         port rc=-1\n    0022 EL_GET terminal   oracle rc=0 <dumb>   port rc=-1 <(null)>\n\nLine 0007 is the useful one: both reject it, so the entry point is reached and the argument is read. Only the dispatch is missing.\n\ncrates/nshedit-abi/src/eln.rs:429 says so itself — \"Step 2 and the whole dispatch are unwritten, so every op takes the C's default arm\" — and the comment below it already specifies what each arm owes, in the order the arguments are read, with the core items it calls. That comment is the spec for this node; it was written when c_variadic was not yet available, which is why the arms were left. It is available now.\n\nThe differential is RED until this lands, and that is deliberate rather than something to route around: the harness reporting that the port does not match is the harness working.\n\nThis is also why the six sibling translate nodes closed alongside core-api-translate deserve re-checking. All of them were closed on a grep for `todo!()` plus the wave-2 gate at 650/650, and neither catches a stub that has a real body and a correct-looking return. conformance-driver-readline and conformance-driver-ub are the instruments for that."
                    }

