            {
                id spec-corrections-2
                title "Correct the rules the ABI pass and the first conformance run proved wrong"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T10:58:32.379154494Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Accumulated since spec-rule-corrections closed. Each was found by doing something rather than by re-reading, which is why they were not in the first pass. Verify each against the C before editing, as before — two of these are second-hand.\n\n1. sem:histedit.wcsdup-fn is wrong about the C. VERIFIED BY ME, not second-hand. docs/spec/port/src/histedit.md:1635-1637 says the bundled implementation \"is only compiled in that case\", i.e. when the system lacks wcsdup. It always compiles: src/wcsdup.c:14 is `#ifndef HAVE_WCSDUP` and :16 is `#include \"config.h\"`, so the guard is evaluated before the macro is defined and is unconditionally true. config.h.in:196 has the macro and src/histedit.h:323 guards the DECLARATION with it correctly, so header and source disagree — the declaration disappears on a system that has wcsdup while the definition stays. Consequence: libedit's own wcsdup overrides glibc's for everything that links it. Found by building the oracle.\n\n2. sem:histedit.el-wset-fn and sem:histedit.el-wget-fn give the four prompt arms' argument type as el_wpfunc_t, a type the C never declares. Grep finds it only in a comment at histedit.h:152, which spells a pair of names — el_pfunct_t / el_wpfunct_t — that nothing declares either. src/prompt.h:44 declares exactly one prompt typedef, el_pfunc_t, and src/el.c uses it in all four arms (lines 240, 262, 471, 477). The rule invented a wide/narrow pair by analogy; prompts have no narrow variant because the prompt is stored wide either way. Check whether the same false analogy reached any other rule describing a narrow entry point. Already fixed in the code comments at crates/nshedit-abi/src/histedit.rs; the rules still carry it.\n\n3. sem:histedit.el-wset-fn's EL_TERMINAL arm says it \"Returns 0 on success, -1 if the display arrays could not be allocated\". It also returns -1 whenever the capability lookup failed, however usable the dumb-terminal fallback is. That is ERR-terminal-22, registered against sem:terminal.terminal-set-fn but not against this rule.\n\n4. ERR-readline-35 asserts rl_point and rl_end are byte offsets without qualification. True only of _rl_update_pos, which computes from el_line, the narrow LineInfo. fn_complete2 declares `const LineInfoW *li` and computes over wchar_t, so the copy a completion callback sees is a CHARACTER offset. Qualify the sentence and cross-reference ERR-completion-13, which already records the wide producer. This is a correction, not a new entry — an earlier proposal to add one was withdrawn as a duplicate.\n\n5. crates/nshedit/src/map.rs:1101-1103 carries a comment repeating the map_init_nls claim that spec-rule-corrections corrected away (\"they survive only as the ESC-prefixed keymacros map_init_meta just made\"). The code is right — it queries iswprint — only the prose is stale. Code comment, not a rule, but it belongs in the same sweep.\n\nNot a rule error but the same class of problem, worth deciding here: histedit.h documents H_NEXT_EVDATA, H_DELDATA and H_REPLACE in terms of histdata_t, a type it never declares — it exists only in editline/readline.h:68. A consumer including only histedit.h cannot name it. Possibly inside ERR-core-api-34's annotation-error group; unconfirmed."
                    }

