            {
                id errata-reconcile-2
                title "Reconcile the register against the ABI pass, the first conformance run and the withdrawals"
                deps (spec-corrections-2)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T10:58:55.169931851Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Second reconciliation pass. The first one was accurate when written; these went stale as work landed, plus new entries and three withdrawals.\n\nSTALE STATUS LINES — the entry point they describe is no longer a core_gap panic, as of commit e7ddcd7 (abi-visibility):\n- ERR-modes-27 and ERR-core-api-07 (map_bind's argc overwrite, el_wset's collection loop). Now defined as \"always terminate\" via a list_args helper whose returned length is the C's i. BOTH halves of the C's over-read are now unreachable rather than reproduced: a Vec ends where it ends, and el_wset's fixed arity means there is no caller argument list to run off.\n- ERR-core-api-08 — the EL_EDITOR and EL_WORDCHARS halves now reject NULL with -1. Only the EL_SETFP half stays open, blocked on abi-file-surface.\n- ERR-core-api-14 — now observable through el_wget and reproduced end to end.\n- ERR-core-api-16 — reachable now.\n- The summary paragraph at roughly line 113.\n\nSTALE, AND DISPROVEN BY EXECUTION rather than by reading:\n- ERR-history-17's status (docs/errata.md:1301) says \"the ABI H_FUNC opcode is still a core_gap(...) panic ... so no C caller reaches it yet\". It is not. The first conformance run drives H_FUNC through the port's fixed-arity history_w with all eleven varargs and it matches the oracle exactly, including reproducing the defect itself (callbacks receive something other than the installed ref, is-installed-ref=0 on both sides).\n\nNEW ENTRIES:\n- MB_CUR_MAX divergence, documented on the new el::mb_cur_max. The C's follows setlocale; the port's follows the environment, because crate::locale cannot call setlocale and records why. Observable across the ABI: a C application that never calls setlocale under a UTF-8 LANG gets NARROW_HISTORY cleared by el_wset(EL_HIST) where the port leaves it set. Rule: sem:histedit.el-wset-fn's EL_HIST arm. Reach: any C consumer that skips setlocale, common in small programs.\n- rl_terminal_name is written back as a borrowed pointer — the caller's own buffer, getenv(\"TERM\")'s environment string, or the literal \"dumb\" — under a declared type of char * that invites a write, making two of the three cases undefined and free() a corruption. Same call site passes char ** where el_get reads const char **.\n- Seventeen further inert exports beyond ERR-readline-54's list. Four of them default to 0 where GNU's default to 1, so an untouched consumer sees different behaviour before doing anything.\n- rl_already_prompted's direction is reversed relative to GNU: there the application sets it to suppress a redisplay, here libedit writes it and reads it nowhere, so a consumer using the GNU idiom gets a duplicated prompt. Nothing clears it in callback mode.\n- emacs_meta_keymap is defined twice in one translation unit (readline.c:93 and :137). Both tentative, so C merges them and nothing observable changes — but a declaration-by-declaration translation emits two definitions and fails to link.\n- src/wcsdup.c's always-true guard (see spec-corrections-2 item 1). A C defect in no entry under any id.\n\nWITHDRAWALS — proposed during readline-globals-rules, then found to be duplicates when the WHOLE register was checked rather than its readline section. Do not re-add:\n- The rl_point/rl_end unit disagreement is ERR-completion-13, cited by id at the very site that produces it.\n- The completion-append-character condition is ERR-completion-14.\n- The getc(stdin) half of the query-items entry is ERR-completion-12; only the (size_t) cast remains uncovered.\n- ERR-readline-61 narrows to the ownership contract alone; the leak half is ERR-readline-18.\n\nNOT errata, and must not become entries: the six divergences the first conformance run found are port-side gaps, not C defects. The register is C defects only. The narrow-instantiation aborts belong to abi-narrow and the H_SAVE_FP zero-byte writes to abi-file-surface."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T11:16:42.226980502Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Additions from spec-corrections-2, now committed as 55258de. These supplement the list already on this node.\n\n1. ERR-terminal-22 — extend the `rule:` line with [spec:libedit:sem:histedit.el-wset-fn] and [spec:libedit:sem:el.el-wset-fn]. Both now state the extra -1 return path in their EL_TERMINAL arms. Previously only sem:terminal.terminal-set-fn was registered, even though the entry's own prose already said \"el_wset(EL_TERMINAL, ...) propagates that\".\n\n2. The wcsdup entry this node already plans now has a rule to cite: [spec:libedit:sem:histedit.wcsdup-fn] describes it in full as of 55258de. Suggested class: divergence (build). Reach: HOT, not latent — on any glibc host libedit.so exports a `T wcsdup` with default visibility that shadows libc's for every caller in a process linking libedit ahead of libc, while histedit.h withholds the prototype on exactly those hosts. Disposition: not reproduced, under dec:libedit:posix-only-scope. Measured, not read: the built oracle's config.h has `#define HAVE_WCSDUP 1` and `nm -D` on its .so shows `T wcsdup` anyway.\n\n3. NEW, a sibling of the above: src/reallocarr.c:32 is `#if !HAVE_REALLOCARR` with `#include \"config.h\"` at :34 — the identical always-true guard, and the oracle exports `T reallocarr` too. Unobservable on hosts that lack reallocarr, which is why it has gone unnoticed, but it is the same C defect. src/strlcpy.c is the control: config.h at :20, guard at :36, correct ordering, and correspondingly NOT exported. Either a second clause on the wcsdup entry or its own id. No rule covers reallocarr at all, so if it gets an id it needs a rule too — that is a spec-corrections item, not just a register one.\n\n4. ERR-core-api-34 needs NO change for histdata_t. It was a false alarm: sem:histedit.history-fn (histedit.md:1261-1263) already documents that histedit.h names a type it never defines, and ERR-core-api-34 (errata.md:2273) already names it verbatim.\n\nAlso, one stale code comment for whoever next owns crates/nshedit-abi/src/histedit.rs — not for this node, and left alone deliberately because that file was held by another agent: lines 846-850 say the -1-on-lookup-failure path is something \"sem:histedit.el-wset-fn does not say\". As of 55258de the rule does say it, so that clause is now false."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T11:29:56.55149255Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Further stale status lines, from abi-file-surface landing as 6912ead. Supplements the two lists already on this node.\n\n- ERR-history-19 and ERR-history-20 both say \"Not yet reachable across the ABI: history_save_fp returns -1\". Both are now reachable and reproduced end to end, byte-identical to the oracle in the differential. ERR-history-20's at_start now comes from a real ftell in nshedit-abi's cstdio module, which is the whole point of that entry -- on a pipe or socket ftell returns -1, so the file comes out headerless and history_load later rejects it. That is now genuinely exercised rather than unreachable.\n\n- ERR-core-api-08 says the EL_SETFP arm is a core_gap panic. It is implemented, with NULL defined as descriptor -1.\n\n- ERR-core-api-06's status says the -1-descriptor half is \"over-applied ... every el_init gets descriptor -1 ... blocked on the platform layer\". No longer true. Only NULL or descriptor-less streams get -1 now, so the C's outcome is genuinely reproduced rather than approximated. This one is worth reading carefully -- the status was describing a port limitation as if it were the C's behaviour.\n\nNot errata, recorded so it is not mistaken for one: plan/decisions/platform-layer.md group 12 (\"fileno is unreachable...\") is now stale except for its tcgetattr half. The file's statement that the surface \"is nshedit-abi's alone\" stays correct. Fixing that group belongs with platform-build, not here.\n\nOne measurement from the same run, for whoever owns the terminal concern rather than this node: EL_TELLTC output differs between oracle and port in the CAPABILITY NAMES ONLY -- al/bl/cd/ce/ch against il1/bel/ed/el/hpa. That is termcap spelling against terminfo spelling, i.e. dec:libedit:terminal-caps-via-term-crate, and it is a real observable difference for any consumer that parses telltc output. It did not show as a differential divergence because the current driver does not exercise EL_TELLTC; the next driver (conformance-driver-el) will, so expect it and do not treat it as new."
                    }

