            {
                id nshterm-hashed-db
                title "Survey whether any platform we target ships hashed terminfo, then decide whether to read it"
                priority 0
                deps (nshterm-extract)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T11:53:53.861057785Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Upstream refuses this outright. term-1.2.1's searcher.rs opens with its own module doc: \"ncurses-compatible database discovery — Does not support hashed database, only filesystem!\" So every consumer of that crate silently fails on a hashed system, and nobody was going to fix it because the crate has been looking for a maintainer since November 2018 (Stebalien/term#93).\n\nWhy it matters here rather than being a nicety: the failure is total, not partial. get_dbpath_for_term walks TERMINFO, $HOME/.terminfo, TERMINFO_DIRS and the four compiled-in directories, then within each tries <dir>/<first-char>/<term> and <dir>/<hex>/<term>. On a system whose terminfo is a single terminfo.db, none of those paths exist, tgetent reports \"no entry\", and libedit falls back to dumb defaults — no cursor addressing, no clear-to-eol, no insert/delete line. The whole editor degrades, on a machine where the C works perfectly through ncurses.\n\nDebian uses the filesystem tree, which is why the port works here and why this has not bitten. That makes it a portability cliff rather than a visible bug: it is invisible until it is total.\n\nWhat it costs: ncurses' hashed database is a Berkeley DB 1.85 hash file. Reading it means implementing that format — bucket pages, the hash function, overflow chains — or finding a pure-Rust crate that already does and checking its licence, which is the same trap terminfo 0.9.0's WTFPL was. Do not assume a crate exists; check, and check the licence before designing around it. Writing it is bounded but it is not a weekend.\n\nCheck before building: which systems actually ship hashed. ncurses is configured with --with-hashed-db and the answer differs by distro and by BSD. That survey decides whether this is P1 or a later nicety, and it is cheap to do first.\n\nWorth knowing for the crate's own sake: this is the feature that makes nshterm better than what we forked rather than merely unblocked. Combined with the termcap column (nshterm-termcap-names), it is a terminfo reader that handles both database layouts and all three name columns, which is more than term or the WTFPL terminfo crate offer. If we publish, that is the argument for it existing."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T12:04:08.758724786Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Candidate found and assessed: https://github.com/jperkin/db185 — a Berkeley DB 1.85 reader/writer in pure Rust, published on crates.io, written for pkgsrc-rs by a NetBSD/pkgsrc developer.\n\nPROBABLY THE WRONG ACCESS METHOD, and this needs checking before anyone designs around it. Its own documentation says \"Hash and recno access methods are out of scope\" — it implements btree only, building a new file from scratch with each key/value pair fitting in a single ~4 KiB page. ncurses' hashed terminfo database uses DB_HASH, which is what \"hashed database\" names. If that is right, db185 does not cover our case at all.\n\nFlagged as unverified rather than settled: the DB_HASH claim is from recollection of ncurses' hashed_db.c and could not be checked here — this host has no terminfo.db and no ncurses source. VERIFY IT FIRST. It is the difference between db185 being a dependency and being a curiosity.\n\nA second complication for the same survey. The on-disk format depends on which libdb ncurses was linked against: --with-hashed-db takes whatever is available and ncurses handles API generations from 1.85 up through DB 4 and 5. So \"hashed terminfo\" is not one format, and a FreeBSD box and a Linux box configured that way may not agree. db185 being 1.85-specific may therefore be doubly narrow — wrong access method AND one generation among several. Establish which generations actually appear in the wild as part of the survey this node already calls for.\n\nWhere it stays useful regardless: it is proof the format is tractable in pure Rust rather than a C-linking problem, and its page-level work — page structures, byte-order handling, the single-page constraint — is a genuine reference for the hash method too. Check its licence before lifting anything, for the reason terminfo 0.9.0's WTFPL already taught us once."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T12:07:01.833918144Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Verified against ncurses source and this host, replacing the recollection the earlier notes on this node were resting on. Two claims held; a third measurement reprioritises the whole node.\n\nCONFIRMED, from ncurses/tinfo/hashed_db.c: the access method is DB_HASH in every branch, so db185 -- btree only by its own documentation -- does not fit. Also confirmed that ncurses spans API generations, and the branches are explicit:\n\n    HASHED_DB_API >= 4   db_create() + result->open(result, NULL, path, NULL, DB_HASH, ...)\n    HASHED_DB_API >= 3   db_create() + result->open(result, path, NULL, DB_HASH, ...)\n    HASHED_DB_API >= 2   db_open(path, DB_HASH, ...)\n    HASHED_DB_API <  2   dbopen(path, ..., DB_HASH, NULL)\n\nThe `< 2` branch is the 1.85 API, so db185 is the right generation for it and only the wrong access method. Extending db185 with hash support would cover exactly that branch and no other. Worth knowing if we ever contribute there rather than build our own.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT CHANGES THE PRIORITY: Debian's libtinfo.so.6 records NEEDED libc.so.6 and nothing else, and has zero db_*/dbopen undefined symbols. Debian's ncurses is built WITHOUT --with-hashed-db. There is no hashed database on the target platform at all.\n\nSo this node is not the cliff the earlier note called it. Re-read that note with this in mind: \"the failure is total, not partial\" and \"the whole editor degrades\" are both accurate about a hashed system and irrelevant to Debian, and I wrote them before checking whether Debian was one. Deprioritised accordingly -- a portability gap for systems configured differently, not something the port is standing on.\n\nWhat the survey still owes, and it is smaller now: which platforms in our POSIX scope actually ship --with-hashed-db. FreeBSD and the commercial Unixes are the candidates. If the answer is none we care about, this leaf can be retired rather than built, and the honest version of nshterm's publishing pitch drops from \"handles both database layouts\" to \"handles the filesystem layout, like ncurses does on Linux\"."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T00:57:09.504672435Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
